    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Director--Institute-of-Transport-and-Logistics-Studies_0156399-2": {
      "title": "Director, Institute of Transport and Logistics Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nShape the future direction of one of the world’s premier transport and logistics research institutes, delivering meaningful impact across academia, industry, government and the broader community. The successful candidate will be appointed to a continuing substantive level E (Professorial) role in the School of International Business, Logistics and Supply Chains and seconded for five years to the Director of ITLS role on an FTE basis commensurate with the directorship duties. Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation. About the opportunity The Institute of Transport and Logistics Studies (ITLS) is one of the world's leading institutes for transport, logistics and supply chain research, education and policy engagement. Ranked #1 in Australia and #8 globally for Transportation Science and Technology (Shanghai Ranking 2025), ITLS has built an international reputation for research excellence, policy influence and industry engagement over almost four decades. With strong partnerships across government, industry and international organisations, and a track record of shaping transport, infrastructure and logistics policy and practice, ITLS offers a rare opportunity to lead a globally recognised institute with significant impact beyond academia. As Director, ITLS, you will provide strategic, academic and operational leadership for this globally recognised research institute within the University of Sydney Business School.  You will advance research excellence, education, industry and government engagement, and organisational performance, positioning ITLS as a leading source of evidence and expertise across transport, infrastructure, logistics and supply chain management. In this role, you will: provide strategic, academic and operational leadership for ITLS, setting its direction and aligning its priorities with those of the Business School and University foster research excellence by supporting high-impact research, external funding, research translation and interdisciplinary collaboration across transport, infrastructure, logistics and supply chain management build and strengthen strategic partnerships with government, industry, professional bodies, alumni and international collaborators to extend the Institute’s impact, profile and reputation coordinate and contribute to high-quality teaching and learning, including the successful supervision of higher degree research students oversee the Institute’s planning, governance, compliance and continuous improvement, ensuring effective resource management, reporting and delivery of strategic priorities support the development and delivery of ITLS’s specialist teaching and executive education offerings, research training and workforce capability development foster an inclusive culture of innovation, collaboration and academic excellence manage the Institute’s financial, human and physical resources to support its sustainability and organisational effectiveness represent and promote ITLS across academic, industry, government and community forums, positioning it as a trusted source of impartial, evidence-based research champion work addressing emerging challenges in sustainable transport, mobility, infrastructure, logistics and resilient supply chains, with meaningful impact on policy, practice and society. About you (skills, capabilities and experience)  You will be an accomplished academic leader with a PhD in a field relevant to ITLS, an established national and international research reputation, and the capacity to lead a high-performing institute. You will bring: deep knowledge of the strategic, policy, industry and academic issues affecting transport, infrastructure, logistics or supply chain management a distinguished record of research and teaching, together with the capacity to provide outstanding academic leadership and articulate a clear strategic direction experience developing and leading a high-performing academic centre, research group or comparable knowledge-intensive organisation established national and international networks across academia, industry, government and the professions a strong record of engagement with government, industry and external stakeholders an understanding of how research can inform policy, regulation, industry practice and public value experience managing operational priorities, governance, budgets, reporting and performance in a complex academic environment strong communication, negotiation and stakeholder management skills experience supporting interdisciplinary collaboration across academic disciplines, professional teams and external partners a commitment to sustainability, equity and public value in transport, logistics, infrastructure and supply chain systems demonstrated experience delivering high-quality teaching and learning, including higher degree research supervision. Key benefits  the opportunity to work within one of Australia’s leading universities a University contribution of 17% of your base salary to your superannuation fund tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund generous relocation allowance if moving from interstate or overseas  access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres.  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have the right to work in Australia before commencing employment. Visa sponsorship may be available for the successful candidate if required. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be appointed into a continuing substantive level E (Professorial) role in the School of International Business, Logistics and Supply Chains and seconded for five years to the Director of ITLS role on an FTE basis commensurate with the directorship duties. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  To meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to have engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications, including a CV and cover letter of no more than four pages addressing the requirements of the role, should be submitted through the Odgers website. Please refer to the candidate information brochure and position description at the bottom for further details. The preferred method of application is online at https://www.odgers.com/en-au/opportunities/ . All applications will receive an automated response. Should you be unable to submit your application online, please email it to: Sophie.Malone@odgers.com For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. The University has engaged executive search firm Odgers to assist with this appointment who will support the Selection Committee to identify the widest possible field of qualified candidates and to assess candidates against the requirements for the role. For a confidential conversation, please contact: Sophie Malone on Sophie.Malone@odgers.com or +61 2 8905 3703 Applications close at 11.59pm AEDT on Wednesday 14 October 2026. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Click to view the Candidate Information Pack for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 14 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Concord/Director--ANZAC-Research-Institute_0155786-3": {
      "title": "Director, ANZAC Research Institute",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity for an outstanding senior academic who is a leading authority within their discipline Fixed term position for 5 years with opportunity for further appointment with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The ANZAC Research Institute is a biomedical research institute where discovery meets care. Guided by our history, we partner with clinicians to innovate and deliver improved health outcomes for Defence members, veterans and our broader community. The Institute was established in 1995 and is an entity of Sydney Local Health District (SLHD) operating as a research partnership with the University of Sydney. It is located on the campus of Concord Repatriation General Hospital (CRGH), a primary referral hospital within SLHD and a teaching hospital of the University of Sydney. We are seeking to appoint an outstanding academic at the level of Associate Professor or Professor (Level D or E) as the Director of the ANZAC Research Institute. The appointee will have overall responsibility for leading the implementation of the Institutes strategic plan, provide scientific guidance, and liaise with SLHD and Precinct Academic Directors on operational and financial management of the Institute. In accordance with the research collaborative agreement, the Director will have accountability to the Chief Executive of SLHD and the Dean of Faculty of Medicine and Health University of Sydney (or their appointed delegates from time to time) as designated employers of the Director. In the case of the Faculty of Medicine and Health, the Director will report to the Head of School Medical Sciences, one of seven schools in FMH. The Director will also provide regular reports to the ANZAC Advisory Council covering the overall research performance of the Institute. The position is to be based in the School of Medical Sciences at University of Sydney and the Director will be a member of the school’s leadership team. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The successful candidate will: hold research doctoral degree (PhD, MD or equivalent) with an outstanding record of achievement in a field of biomedical research relevant to the Institute (aging or related health specialties). have proven ability to deliver research an international standard in one or more fields relevant to the research of the Institute. have an outstanding research track record as evidenced by publications, instances of research translation and/or commercialisation supervision of successful HDR students. demonstrate evidence of peer recognition through involvement and leading committees, boards and editorial appointments and track record of developing national & international collaboration. have proven ability to secure significant and sustained competitive research funding. have proven ability to deliver on strategic directions of the Institute and provide scientific leadership within a complex biomedical research environment. Capacity to promote the research and translation functions of the Institute with the SLHD and University. understand research governance policies and legislation and rules of ethical research conduct. promote a strong research culture, strengthening existing collaborations and fostering new partnerships. be managing engagement with industry partners. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and Relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a 5-year fixed term full-time position, subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees.  There is opportunity for further appointments subject to performance. The successful candidate will be offered an attractive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Other As this position is located on a NSW Health site, it is a regulatory requirement to complete compulsory employment checks prior to starting in this position. For more information and instructions, please visit: NSW Health employment requirements Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure for further details. Candidate Information Brochure For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Intending applicants are welcome to seek further information about the position from: Professor Mary Collins, Head of School, School of Medical Sciences, mary.collins@sydney.edu.au  Faculty of Medicine and Health. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact: Dr Mushtaq Sobhan (Senior Talent Partner, Recruitment Operations, Human Resources) by email mushtaq.sobhan@sydney.edu.au   Applications Close Friday 09 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Associate-Vice-President--Sydney-Future-Students_0157138-1": {
      "title": "Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUniversity of Sydney The University of Sydney is seeking an exceptional leader to join its External Engagement portfolio as Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students. Reporting to the Vice-President, External Engagement, this influential executive role provides strategic leadership of the University's student recruitment and admissions functions and plays a critical role in delivering the University's growth ambitions, student experience objectives and revenue goals.  The Opportunity Higher education is undergoing significant transformation. Changing student expectations, increasing domestic and international competition, advances in artificial intelligence and digital engagement, evolving government policy settings and demographic shifts are reshaping the future student landscape. As Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students, you will lead the University's student recruitment and admissions strategy, ensuring the University continues to attract and enrol outstanding students from Australia and around the world. Working closely with senior leaders across the institution, you will provide strategic leadership and market insights to support decision-making, strengthen competitive positioning and drive sustainable growth. This is a rare opportunity to lead one of the most strategically important functions within Australia's leading university, with responsibility for shaping the future student pipeline and contributing directly to the University's long-term success. Key Responsibilities Working closely with the Vice-President, External Engagement, Deans, Deputy Vice-Chancellors and senior leaders across the University, you will: Lead the development and execution of the University's student recruitment and admissions strategy. Drive domestic and international recruitment performance, with significant responsibility for University revenue, while balancing sustainable growth with the University's broader strategic priorities. Provide strategic advice and market intelligence to inform institutional planning and decision-making. Lead the delivery of a high-performing, service-oriented student recruitment and admissions function. Utilise market insights, competitive intelligence and emerging trends to identify growth opportunities and strengthen market position. Develop and maintain strong partnerships across faculties, portfolios and external stakeholders. Lead organisational performance, financial planning, governance, risk management and compliance across the portfolio. Influence policy development and strategic responses to changes in the higher education and regulatory environment. Champion continuous improvement and innovation to enhance the prospective student experience. Leadership Impact This role sits at the centre of the University's growth strategy and student experience agenda. The successful candidate will lead a significant and highly visible function responsible for recruitment, admissions and market intelligence, while building strong relationships across a complex stakeholder environment that includes Faculties, professional services leaders, external partners and government stakeholders. You will bring the credibility, influence and leadership capability required to align diverse stakeholders around shared objectives, drive organisational performance and deliver meaningful outcomes for students and the University. The portfolio also encompasses the University's global mobility and exchange function, providing an opportunity to shape and strengthen the University's international student experience and global engagement agenda. About You (skills, capabilities and experience) You are an accomplished executive leader with a track record of delivering strategic growth outcomes in a complex organisation. You will bring: Significant leadership experience in student recruitment, admissions, marketing, customer acquisition, commercial growth, student experience or a related field. A demonstrated ability to develop and execute large-scale growth and engagement strategies. Strong commercial and financial acumen, with experience managing budgets, performance targets and business planning. Exceptional stakeholder engagement and influencing skills. Experience leading large, complex teams through change and transformation. A strong understanding of data, analytics and market intelligence to support strategic decision-making. The ability to operate effectively in a highly complex and matrixed environment. Outstanding communication, leadership and organisational skills. While experience in higher education is highly regarded, applications are encouraged from candidates in adjacent sectors who have led large-scale customer acquisition, admissions, student engagement, commercial growth, marketing or service operations functions. Why Join the University of Sydney? As Australia's first university and one of the world's leading research-intensive institutions, the University of Sydney is committed to excellence in education, research and community impact. The Associate Vice President, Sydney Future Students will play a pivotal role in shaping the University's future growth and student community. This position offers the opportunity to influence institutional strategy, lead a critical enterprise-wide function and contribute to the future of higher education at both a national and global level. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a continuing position on a full-time basis. The appointment is subject to completion of a satisfactory probation period for new appointees. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Dr Courtenay Atwell Korn Ferry Email: Courtenay.atwell@kornferry.com © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Tuesday 13 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Electrochemical-Engineering_0156960-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrochemical Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 1.5 years  Apply your expertise in electrochemical device development and chemical process design to advance sustainable technologies Base Salary $113,634 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at the University of Sydney, established in 1946, offered the first university-level chemical engineering program in Australia. The School is committed to teaching excellence and our graduates are highly regarded and sought after by employers in Australia and overseas. The School is research-intensive and considered a world leader in many cutting edge areas of chemical and biomolecular engineering. This is an exciting opportunity from the School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering for a  Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrochemical Engineering to work in the Electrochemical Engineering Lab led by Dr Fengwang Li . This position offers an opportunity to contribute to an applied research program focused on electrochemical technologies for sustainable chemical production and energy conversion. You will combine hands-on experimentation with device and process engineering to develop, evaluate and improve electrochemical reactors and their supporting systems. We are particularly interested in candidates with practical experience in electrolysers or fuel cells who can translate engineering concepts into working experimental systems. The role will involve device design and assembly, electrochemical performance and durability testing, process development, and contributions to scale-up and system integration. You will work collaboratively with researchers, students and technical specialists, contributing both to fundamental understanding and to the development of reliable, scalable electrochemical technologies.  Your key responsibilities will be to: Design, assemble and improve electrochemical cells and reactor prototypes, including the selection and integration of electrodes, membranes, flow-field components and sealing systems. Develop, commission and operate experimental test systems, integrating electrochemical devices with fluid-handling equipment, instrumentation and data acquisition. Plan and conduct electrochemical experiments to evaluate performance, efficiency and durability, identify limitations and failure mechanisms, and implement evidence-based improvements. Apply chemical process engineering principles to develop process configurations, perform material and energy balances, prepare process flow diagrams and piping and instrumentation diagrams, and select appropriate equipment. Contribute to device scale-up and system integration, addressing practical challenges in flow distribution, heat and mass transfer, sealing, reproducibility and stable operation. Translate device concepts into CAD models and engineering drawings, and coordinate component fabrication, procurement and assembly as required. Analyse and interpret experimental data, maintain reproducible research records and engineering documentation, and prepare technical reports and research publications in accordance with confidentiality and intellectual property requirements. Collaborate effectively with colleagues and research partners, support student training and mentoring, and contribute to risk assessments, operating procedures and safe laboratory practices. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Electrochemical Engineering who has: A PhD degree (or near completion) in Chemical Engineering, Electrochemical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Materials Science, Chemistry or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated hands-on experience with electrolysers or fuel cells, including device assembly, operation, performance testing and troubleshooting. Demonstrated ability to design electrochemical devices or associated experimental hardware, including the use of CAD software to produce models and engineering drawings, with consideration of materials compatibility, fabrication, sealing and assembly. Demonstrated capability in chemical process design for experimental or prototype systems, including material and energy balances, process flow diagrams, piping and instrumentation diagrams, and equipment selection. A strong understanding of electrochemical performance and relevant transport processes, together with practical experience in electrochemical characterisation, performance evaluation and durability testing. The ability to plan and deliver experimental research independently, resolve practical engineering problems, critically analyse data and maintain accurate technical records. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with research achievements appropriate to career stage and opportunity, demonstrated through publications, validated prototypes, patents or substantive technical reports. The ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary team, together with a commitment to safe working practices, research integrity and the responsible handling of confidential information and intellectual property. The following experience would be highly regarded: Scaling electrochemical devices from laboratory cells to larger-area devices, multi-cell stacks or integrated prototype systems. Electrode fabrication, membrane electrode assembly preparation, or integration of gas diffusion electrodes into working devices. Computational fluid dynamics or multiphysics modelling to support device design and interpretation of experimental results, using COMSOL, ANSYS Fluent or equivalent tools. Process simulation or techno-economic assessment using Aspen Plus, Aspen HYSYS or equivalent approaches. Automated experimental testing, instrument control or data acquisition using tools such as LabVIEW, Python or MATLAB. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Consultants at recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 11 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Geology_0156260-2": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Geology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, Fixed term until 31 December 2029 on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in Geology Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 p.a  + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Geology. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of geology while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research using data science tools/methodologies applied to geology and geochemistry. Fieldwork experience and a research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards critical mineral exploration is highly desirable. The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of critical minerals and the energy transition. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in geology effectively in the classroom, lab, and field, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: Contribute to knowledge within geology and geochemistry engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences (at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels) which meet the University’s expectations and standards for education design and deliver quality educational programs and experiences that support student needs and meet curriculum requirements influence outstanding teaching and learning outcomes contribute to research, education, and administrative activities develop an academic profile through conferences, seminars and industry engagement enhance a positive workplace culture and strategic direction of the University.  The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those domains. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability. The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community. To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer in Geology who has: a PhD in geology or a cognate discipline a combination of skills across data science tools/methods and fieldwork the demonstrated ability to carry out high quality teaching in geology, particularly in the area of critical minerals and energy transitions a high level of interpersonal skills including the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues good organisational and administrative skills with attention to detail a high level of written and verbal communication skills an ability to engage productively with partners both within and outside of the University to develop and promote their teaching and research activities. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-Igneous-Geology--Women-Only-_0157069-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer Igneous Geology (Women Only)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in geology Base Salary Level B, $132,533 - $157,381 p.a + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This position is exempt under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977, to conduct targeted recruitment programs for women. The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Igneous Geology. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of geology while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research using methodologies applied to igneous petrology and/or igneous geochemistry. Fieldwork experience is essential and a research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards external collaboration with industry and government is highly desirable. The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of igneous petrology. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in geology effectively in the classroom, field and laboratory, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver high-quality teaching in geology and cross-disciplinary settings across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels, in classroom, field and laboratory environments design and deliver engaging educational programs and learning experiences that respond to student needs and meet curriculum requirements lead and undertake fieldwork, including the collection of field data to enrich teaching build partnerships with industry, academic and government organisations to advance teaching, learning and research outcomes develop a research profile appropriate to career stage and opportunity, evidenced through high-quality publications and success in attracting external research funding contribute effectively to administrative and service activities across the School foster a positive, inclusive and collaborative workplace culture support the strategic direction of the School, Faculty and University. The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those subdisciplines. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability. The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community. To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer in Igneous Geology who has: PhD in geology, with specialisation in igneous petrology, Earth materials and/or magmatic processes knowledge of igneous petrology and igneous geochemistry, and familiarity with metamorphic processes skills spanning light and electron microscopy, mineral chemistry, thermobarometry and phase equilibrium application, or skills in other relevant analytical or interpretive methods holds a current driver’s license. Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-Geology--Geophysics-or-Earth-Science--Women-Only-_0155683": {
      "title": "Lecturer Geology, Geophysics or Earth Science (Women Only)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Geosciences Opportunity to make valuable contributions to teaching and research in geology Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 p.a + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This position is exempt under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977, to conduct targeted recruitment programs for women. The University of Sydney is seeking applications for a Lecturer in Geology, Geophysics or Earth Science. The primary function of this position is to undertake research and teaching in the field of geology, geophysics or Earth science while also making a significant collaborative contribution to research using methodologies applied to economic geology. Fieldwork experience is essential and a research profile that demonstrates a potential connection towards external collaboration with industry and government is highly desirable. The position has responsibility for teaching courses in new and existing curricula at the undergraduate and postgraduate level under the broad theme of economic geology. The applicant will be expected to teach fundamental and advanced concepts in geology effectively in the classroom, field and laboratory, supported by digital-based approaches including virtual training and teaching. Your key responsibilities will be to: deliver high-quality teaching in geology, geophysics or Earth science and cross-disciplinary settings across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels, in classroom, field and laboratory environments design and deliver engaging educational programs and learning experiences that respond to student needs and meet curriculum requirements lead and undertake fieldwork, including the collection of field data to enrich teaching build partnerships with industry, academic and government organisations to advance teaching, learning and research outcomes develop a research profile appropriate to career stage and opportunity, evidenced through high-quality publications and success in attracting external research funding contribute effectively to administrative and service activities across the School foster a positive, inclusive and collaborative workplace culture support the strategic direction of the School, Faculty and University. The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those subdisciplines. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability. The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community. To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Lecturer in Geology, Geophysics or Earth Science who has: PhD in geology, geophysics or Earth science demonstrated expertise in economic geology, including the formation, distribution and exploration of geological resources skills spanning geophysics, remote-sensing, GeoAI, and/or large data manipulation and analyses holds a current driver’s license.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Postdoctoral-Researcher-in-Microwave-and-Antenna-Engineering-for-CubeSat-Applications_0156859": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Microwave and Antenna Engineering for CubeSat Applications",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term for 2 years with the possibility of extension   Join the School of Electrical and Computer Engineering and contribute to pioneering research  redefining CubeSat technology Base Salary Academic Level A $113,634 - $125,869 p.a or Academic Level B $132,533 - $157,381 p.a. + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Electrical and Computer Engineering is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate/Fellow (Level A or Level B) to contribute to a research project focused on the development of advanced microwave and antenna technologies for CubeSat applications, including additively manufactured and 4D-printed shape-morphing antenna systems. The Postdoctoral Researcher will provide specialist technical expertise in microwave circuits, antenna engineering, electromagnetic modelling, additive manufacturing, materials characterisation and experimental validation. The appointee will work closely with the Chief Investigator (CI), Partner Investigators (PIs), research students and external/internal collaborators to address key engineering challenges and support the technical delivery of the project. Your key responsibilities will be to: Design, model and optimise microwave circuits, antennas and antenna systems for CubeSat and related space applications. Undertake full-wave electromagnetic simulation and numerical modelling of microwave and antenna components using industry-standard electromagnetic simulation software. Develop additively manufactured and 4D-printed antenna prototypes, including the preparation and optimisation of fabrication processes. Conduct electrical, electromagnetic and materials characterisation of 3D- and 4D-printed materials and structures relevant to microwave applications. Perform hands-on fabrication, integration and experimental testing of microwave and antenna hardware. Develop and experimentally demonstrate antenna functions including reconfigurability, shape morphing and two-dimensional beam steering for CubeSat applications. Support system- and component-level development, integration and validation of antenna subsystems. Analyse experimental and simulation results and contribute to design iterations and technical problem solving. Work collaboratively with the CI, PIs, research staff, PhD candidates, Honours students and external project partners. Assist the CI and PIs with the technical supervision and mentoring of PhD and Honours students participating in the project. Provide technical guidance to students undertaking antenna modelling, additive manufacturing, prototyping and experimental measurements. Contribute to research publications, technical reports, presentations, project milestones and other research outputs. Participate in project meetings and communicate research progress effectively to academic and industry collaborators. Contribute to the effective operation of the research laboratory and ensure that experimental activities are undertaken in accordance with relevant laboratory and WHS requirements. About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Postdoctoral Researcher in Microwave and Antenna Engineering for CubeSat Applications who has: A PhD in Electrical Engineering, Electronic Engineering, Microwave Engineering, Antenna Engineering, Electromagnetics, RF Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Strong knowledge of electromagnetic theory, microwave engineering and antenna theory. Demonstrated experience in the design and modelling of microwave circuits and antennas, preferably for high-frequency, satellite, CubeSat or related applications. Demonstrated proficiency with electromagnetic simulation and design software, such as CST Studio Suite, Ansys HFSS, Keysight ADS, or equivalent platforms. Demonstrated hands-on experience in microwave and antenna hardware development, fabrication, prototyping and experimental validation. Experience with 3D printing/additive manufacturing, particularly for microwave, RF, antenna or electronic applications. Experience in the characterisation of materials and/or fabricated components relevant to microwave and antenna applications. Experience in shape memory materials modelling, training and manufacturing Demonstrated capability to undertake both component-level and system-level engineering development. To be considered for appointment at Level B, we’re looking for evidence of: Knowledge of satellite and CubeSat systems, including antenna integration constraints for small satellites. Experience with reconfigurable, deployable, origami-inspired or shape-morphing antenna technologies. Experience with 4D printing, smart materials or shape-memory materials. Experience in microwave materials characterisation, including dielectric and/or conductive material measurements. Experience with antenna measurement systems, including near-field or far-field antenna characterisation. Experience in phased-array antennas, beam steering or electronically/mechanically reconfigurable antenna systems. Experience working on collaborative research projects involving universities, industry partners or international collaborators. Demonstrated publication record in high-quality journals and conferences in microwave engineering, antennas, additive manufacturing or related fields. Previous experience assisting with the supervision or mentoring of postgraduate and undergraduate research students. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 19 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Specialist-in-Small-Animal-Anaesthesia_0157099-1": {
      "title": "Specialist in Small Animal Anaesthesia",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nBe part of building the future of veterinary clinical education at the University of Sydney Full time continuing employment* Located on Camperdown Campus at the Sydney School of Veterinary Science in The University of Sydney Opportunity to inspire and train the next generation of veterinarians, engage with the school ranked # 1 in Australia, within a world-class university Base Salary HEO 9 (38 hours), $165,080 - $174,177 p.a. + 17% superannuation + specialist loading when registered in NSW as a specialist to a total package of up to $234,737 p.a *Continuing contingent on the continuation of external funding The University of Sydney Veterinary Teaching Hospital is looking for a new colleague to work in a growing team supporting the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital and guiding the development of Doctor of Veterinary Medicine students. This role does not require previous academic experience but rather a strong clinical background and interest in supporting student development. About the opportunity Your chance to have a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession as you care for the lives of animals. We are seeking to appoint a Specialist In Small Animal Anaesthesia to join a team of outstanding world-class clinical colleagues at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Sydney. Small animal anaesthesia is a core discipline in the training of the future leaders of the veterinary profession through a globally accredited training program, the Doctor of Veterinary Medicine as well as grow this essential discipline through supporting residency training. The University Veterinary Teaching Hospital is one of the core teaching and research facilities in the Sydney School of Veterinary Science, a globally ranked veterinary school. The academic environment is different to commercial specialty practice with the academic mission of teaching and research being central to operations. In this role, you will take an active role in the training of students working in the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital. Depending on your level you will be expected to assist in and be part of the development & implementation of the Unit’s Strategic Plan actively contributing to its management, including contributing to the financial viability of the Unit. You will also be expected to pursue clinical research to further knowledge of your specialty. The key benefits be part of developing the next generation of veterinarians be stimulated by an academic environment focused on teaching and research opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital opportunity for a different professional pathway where you can leverage your clinical expertise, enhance work life balance, and find purpose in academic opportunities salary packaging available 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board Industry-leading leave provisions including generous paid parental leave up to 38 weeks entitlements (pending eligibility) compressed work week available generous relocation allowance and access to research and publication support access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres discounted Staff Parking available on-site gain the recognition of working in a leading Veterinary School in an outstanding University. We support and encourage excellence in all aspects of the role, from patient care, teaching and learning, and research. The school values highly the tenants of mutual support, respect, empathy, collegiality and integrity. Join an organization that has over a hundred years of experience in clinical training and innovation. Be part of the transformational process supporting students as they transition to become veterinarians. A privileged role for someone who wants more than a job- come and do work with a purpose. Your key responsibilities will be to maintain reputation for academic excellence provide high-quality veterinary clinical service in veterinary anaesthesia services for clients of UVTHS during business hours and contribute to afterhours anaesthesia coverage develop an expanding client base for the hospital’s anaesthesia service participate in provision of education to DVM students and continuing veterinary education provided by the unit coordinate the daily activities in the anaesthesia unit and provide additional support when required for case-management of patients in other units in the UVTHS continually improving the standard of practice provide anaesthesia services to Camden clinic on a rotating basis for equine and small animal clients as required actively participate in meetings, planning and development in the UVTHS at the discretion of the Hospital Director and Hospital Manager. Since its founding in 1910, the mission of the Sydney School of Veterinary Science (SSVS) has been to provide the nation’s best training in the health, welfare, and conservation of animals. The Sydney School of Veterinary Science is internationally accredited with the American Veterinary Medical Association, Canadian Veterinary Medical Association, the Australasian Veterinary Boards Council and Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (UK) by reciprocal agreement. Veterinary Science at Sydney is ranked 1st in Australia, within a world-class university. To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here About you The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Veterinary Specialist in Small Animal Anaesthesia : Veterinary Specialist in Small Animal Anaesthesia (HEO level 9) who is a veterinarian with at least partial responsibility for the management of a unit or division, including the management of a range of staff working within the relevant discipline or sub-discipline, who has: a degree in veterinary science registrable in New South Wales, Australia a Diplomate or Fellowship permitting registration with the NSW Veterinary Scientists Board as a Specialist in small animal anaesthesia. The commencement level will be determined based on the candidate’s skills, experience and knowledge. The 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all the role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   If you are a current employee of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 27 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-AI-Enabled-Atomic-Scale-Materials-Characterisation_0156863": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in AI Enabled Atomic-Scale Materials Characterisation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time 12 month fixed term opportunity Base Salary, Academic Level A (6-8) offering a base salary of $117,936 - $125,896 p.a. + 17% superannuation    Work in a highly collaborative, international, and interdisciplinary research team   About the opportunity   The School of Aerospace Mechanical and Mechatronic Engineering (AMME) is one of Australia’s leading research and teaching Engineering Schools, with degree programs in Mechanical, Mechatronic, Aeronautical and Space Engineering. The school has a student body of over 1,000 students and has seen strong growth in demand for teaching at the undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The school supports teaching and research activities across disciplines including aerospace, combustion, design, fluid mechanics, materials, mechanical, mechatronic and robotics engineering. To learn more about the School click here   We are currently seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in AI Enabled Atomic-Scale Materials Characterisation to work within a highly collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment involving researchers, HDR students and international collaborators across academia and industry.   The successful candidate will undertake research at the intersection of advanced microscopy, atom probe tomography (APT), artificial intelligence and materials science. Working with world-class characterisation facilities, they will develop quantitative and AI-enabled approaches to understand atomic-scale structures, defects and structure-property relationships in advanced materials, contributing to next-generation materials discovery.   The successful candidate will be expected to:   ·       conduct innovative research using advanced microscopy and characterisation techniques to investigate atomic-scale structure, chemistry and materials behaviour ·       develop and apply quantitative, computational and AI-enabled approaches to materials characterisation, data analysis and scientific discovery ·       collaborate with multidisciplinary research teams, industry partners and international collaborators to deliver high-impact research outcomes ·       contribute to student supervision, research training, grant development and the dissemination of research through leading journals and conferences.   About you   ·       a PhD in Materials Science and Engineering, Physics, Materials Characterisation, Computational Materials Science, or a closely related discipline ·       demonstrated expertise in advanced microscopy and microanalysis techniques, particularly atom probe tomography (APT)  ·       experience with complementary characterisation techniques such as TEM, SEM, EBSD and XRD, and the analysis of complex materials datasets ·       proficiency in quantitative analysis, computational methods and programming, with experience in AI, machine learning or data-driven materials research highly desirable ·       a strong publication record, excellent communication skills, and the ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary research teams and supervise research students.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close No End Date"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-Exercise-and-Sport-Sciences--Education-Focused-_0157163-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer-Exercise and Sport Sciences (Education Focused)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\n2-year part time fixed term contract available Be a part of The School of Health Sciences, which is ranked number three in the world for Sports-Related Subjects Base Salary ranging from $117,936- $157,381+ 17% superannuation About the opportunity The Sydney School of Health Sciences is undergoing significant growth and investment as part of the Faculty of Medicine and Health’s strategic vision. We are seeking a Lecturer (Level A/B) with expertise in Exercise and Sport Science. The successful candidate will make a substantial contribution to teaching and learning through the delivery, design, and evaluation of high-quality educational programs. They will also be expected to build their research profile, strengthen their disciplinary expertise, and contribute to research impact within the School and broader academic community. The Sydney School of Health Sciences is ranked among the world’s leading institutions for sport-related disciplines, including sport science, physiotherapy, and rehabilitation. As part of the University of Sydney, which is consistently recognised as one of Australia’s top universities, the School benefits from a strong reputation for excellence in research, teaching, innovation, and sustainability.   The School is seeking to appoint a Lecturer (Education Focused, Level A/B) on a fixed-term basis. Based in the state-of-the-art Susan Wakil Health Building at the Camperdown campus, the successful candidate will contribute to the delivery of teaching across strength and conditioning, sport science, and exercise science units of study. This role offers an exciting opportunity to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning through the development, delivery, and evaluation of high-quality educational experiences. The successful candidate will be a collaborative and engaged academic who is committed to excellence in teaching and ready to contribute across a broad range of academic activities. The position also provides opportunities to contribute to research and scholarship in Exercise and Sport Science as part of a multidisciplinary team within the Faculty of Medicine and Health.   Your key responsibilities include: Undertake educational innovation, curriculum design and review, contributing to outstanding educational delivery and outcomes Engage in high-quality teaching and learning experiences which meet the University’s expectations and standards for education Contribute to the School’s research activities, expertise, and impact. Engage in supervision of higher-degree research students Establish collaborations and partnerships to enhance the research profile and reputation of the university Carry out administrative tasks primarily connected with the area of discipline Contribute to a positive workplace culture in which diversity of thought, contribution, collaboration, excellence and achieving outcomes are valued and enabled Undertake other duties appropriate within the classification level as required.   About you PhD in an academic field relevant to the discipline is preferred Current registration with Exercise and Sport Science Australia (ESSA) as an Accredited Exercise Scientist or Accredited Sports Scientist (ASpS) and /or current registration with Australian Strength and Conditioning Association (ASCA) would be preferred Experience in teaching and learning in a tertiary environment, as evidenced by coordination of units and supervision of students in practicals, on placements and/or in discipline-related simulation teaching Relevant experience and expertise in strength and conditioning and sports science Demonstrated success in designing implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives Demonstrated ability to foster inclusive, equitable and respectful learning environments A developing network of relationships with key academic, clinical and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.      To submit an application for this role please provide the following information: 1. A cover letter which includes a written response to selection criteria addressing your ability to meet the requirements outlined above within the context of the key responsibilities of the role and relevant discipline (maximum 1000 words). 3. Your current CV or resume, including two referees. Please ensure your cover letter is clear, concise, addressing the selection criteria and states the type of employment in which you are applyin g.       For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Ann Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Magma-Research-Associate---Magma-Research-Fellow_0156699-1": {
      "title": "Magma Research Associate / Magma Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n2 x full time, 3-year fixed term positions, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Opportunity to drive research in computational pure mathematics through the development of mathematical software Base Salary, Academic level A/B $113,634 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking up to two Magma Research Associates / Magma Research Fellows to develop and maintain the Magma computer algebra system ( http://magma.maths.usyd.edu.au/magma/ ).   These positions are based at the University of Sydney in a team led by John Voight as part of an international collaboration funded by the Simons Foundation between the University of Sydney and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) in Cambridge, Massachusetts.                                                                                                   Your key responsibilities will be to:   design and implement mathematical algorithms, and facilitate their integration into Magma engage with users, researchers, and developers, both internally at the University of Sydney and externally across multiple institutions publish research papers on mathematical theory and the development and analysis of algorithms explore how artificial intelligence can support the development and use of computer algebra systems, including mathematical programming, software verification and testing, documentation, and new ways for researchers to interact with computational mathematics software compile data sets relevant to research in these areas and integrate them into Magma participate in and support workshops and meetings.   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for Magma Research Associate / Magma Research Fellows who have:   a PhD in mathematics, computer science, or a related discipline a strong background in computational mathematics, computer algebra, or mathematical software, with experience relevant to specific areas such as algebra, arithmetic geometry, number theory, group theory, representation theory, commutative algebra, or algebraic geometry experience in the design, analysis, or implementation of algorithms strong programming skills; experience with Magma or another computer algebra system is highly desirable experience with C/C++ and Linux, or the demonstrated ability to work effectively in comparable systems-programming and Unix environments strong communication skills, both written and verbal the ability to travel and present research results.   Desirable experience includes:   experience developing substantial mathematical or scientific software experience with modern software development practices, including version control, testing, profiling, optimisation, and collaborative development experience with artificial intelligence or machine-learning tools and methods, particularly their application to mathematical research or software development. This may include AI-assisted programming, automated testing and documentation, mathematical reasoning, code analysis, or the development of AI-enabled interfaces and research tools.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 20 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Learning-Success-Advisor--Language-and-Learning-_0152997-2": {
      "title": "Learning Success Advisor (Language and Learning)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing (contingent on funding) Make a meaningful impact on student success by delivering tailored learning support in a collaborative university environment Base Salary $117,133 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Learning Success Advisor is a member of a team of specialist professional staff focusing on student facing support responsible for the facilitation of post-entry academic language and learning support initiatives for all students (domestic and international) and contributes to the University’s strategic initiatives for the provision of learning support.    The Learning Success Advisor works in partnership with faculties, schools, and the library to facilitate the development of discipline specific integrated and embedded programs, develop and evaluate resources for learning skills programs, provide groups and individual learning skills sessions, contribute to the development and delivery of peer-learning initiatives, advise students on personalised learning plans and direct students to relevant learning support resources, monitor student engagement via the centralised learning support portal, administer online diagnostic tools and milestone tasks and contribute to the development of plans, strategies and procedures in line with the University’s strategy.    Your key responsibilities will be to:   work within a dynamic team, reporting to the Learning Hub Lead  design and create learning and teaching materials for the area  contribute to the development, implementation and evaluation of post-entry academic language and learning support initiatives for all students through providing specialist guidance and advice  deliver learning support, advising and training activities to students, peer-facilitators and staff for up to 20 hours a week  work collaboratively with the Learning Hub Lead, Learning Success Advisors and colleagues across CET + Learning Hub to ensure effective contribution to learning support programs and initiatives  contribute to and engage actively with the post-entry academic language and learning community beyond the University to further the reputation of the University.    About you   post graduate qualifications and/or relevant work experience in a related field (e.g., applied linguistics, Higher Education, TESOL)  experience facilitating learning support initiatives within the higher education/knowledge sector  experience in developing and facilitating high-quality, innovative programs to support post-entry academic language and learning  theoretical and practical understanding of effective educational practice in the post-entry, higher education space  demonstrated experience in designing and delivering discipline-specific academic language support programs  experience using learning analytics to inform learning design, support student progress and monitor student outcomes  experience using innovative educational technology platforms to facilitate language and communication support to diverse cohorts of students.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Tooba Fida, Recruitment Operations by email to tooba.fida@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 22 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer--Teaching-and-Research--Behavioural-and-Social-Sciences-in-Health_0157035-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Teaching and Research)-Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nFull time continuing position available Exciting opportunity for a Lecturer to contribute to teaching and research within the Discipline of Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health within in The Sydney School of Health Sciences Base Salary ranging from $132,533- $187,198 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Sydney School of Health Sciences is in a period of exciting and significant investment by the Faculty of Medicine and Health. In order to deliver on the University’s ambitious strategic plan, the University is seeking academics who are passionate about improving health outcomes and/or health equity in the local, national and global communities, who are committed to teaching and research, and area team player ready to contribute across the broad field of academic activities. The Discipline of Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health (BSSH) brings a social science perspective to understanding health and healthcare, exploring the social, cultural, economic and political factors that influence health outcomes and contribute to inequities. The University is seeking to employee a Lecturer/ Senior Lecturer ( Level B or Level C - Teaching and Research) to play a key role in advancing the discipline's strategic focus on health equity, social determinants of health, health services research and research methods education. Working within a collaborative and interdisciplinary environment, the successful candidate will contribute to innovative teaching, impactful research and strong partnerships across the University and external health sectors.   This is a continuing full time 40% (teaching), 40% (research), and 20% (service) position, and offers an opportunity to develop and contribute to research and teaching in The Discipline of Behavioural and Social Sciences in Health (BSSH) as part of a multi-disciplinary team at the Faculty of Medicine and Health.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   Design, coordinate and deliver high-quality teaching across quantitative, qualitative and mixed-methods research approaches Contribute to research methods education across undergraduate, honours and postgraduate programs Coordinate units of study, including assessment design, moderation, quality assurance and management of teaching teams Supervise honours students and contribute to the supervision of Higher Degree by Research (HDR) candidates Support curriculum development, review and integration of research methods teaching across the School Maintain an active and impactful research program aligned with the Discipline's strategic priorities Contribute to research funding applications, scholarly publications and collaborative research initiatives Provide research methods advice and capacity-building opportunities for students and academic colleagues Collaborate with allied health, clinical and interdisciplinary teams to enhance teaching and research outcomes Participate in academic service activities, including committees, student advising, outreach and community engagement.   About you Demonstrated experience teaching university-level research methods across quantitative methodologies Experience in curriculum design, unit coordination and assessment development within higher education Proven ability to supervise or support student research projects at honours and/or postgraduate level Strong knowledge of research design, statistical analysis, quantitative methodologies and mixed-methods approaches Established or emerging research profile, including peer-reviewed publications and research collaborations Experience securing or contributing to competitive research funding is highly desirable Excellent written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to explain complex methodological concepts to diverse audiences Commitment to equity, social justice and culturally safe teaching and research practices Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across disciplinary boundaries and contribute positively to an academic team Ability to engage and support students from diverse backgrounds, including those with limited confidence in research methods and statistics Commitment to continuous improvement, innovation in teaching, and the broader academic life of the Discipline, School and Faculty.   Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. To submit an application for this role please provide the following information: 1. A cover letter which includes a written response to selection criteria addressing your ability to meet the requirements outlined above within the context of the key responsibilities of the role and relevant discipline (maximum 1000 words). 3. Your current CV or resume, including two referees. Please ensure your cover letter is clear, concise, addressing the selection criteria and states the type of employment in which you are applyin g.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi, Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Polymer-Chemistry-and-Hydrogel-Materials_0157297-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymer Chemistry and Hydrogel Materials",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term for 1 year at Camperdown (potential for extension contingent upon funding and performance) Opportunity to contribute to innovative polymer chemistry research Base Salary Level A, $93,424 - $125,896 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Chemistry is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymer Chemistry and Hydrogel Materials to join a dynamic research team focused on the design, synthesis and characterisation of advanced polymeric materials. The successful candidate will undertake cutting-edge research, contribute to high-impact publications, and collaborate with academic and industry partners to advance fundamental and applied polymer science.   This position offers an exciting opportunity to work in a leading research environment with access to state-of-the-art facilities under the supervision of Professor Markus Muellner and to contribute to the University’s research excellence agenda. Click here to learn more School of Chemistry & Polymer Nanostructures Group Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct independent and collaborative research in polymer chemistry and materials science design, synthesise and characterise novel polymeric materials and hydrogels using contemporary synthetic methodologies analyse and interpret experimental data and prepare manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals contribute to the development of research proposals and funding applications present research outcomes at seminars, conferences and stakeholder meetings supervise and provide guidance to higher degree research students where appropriate maintain accurate research records and comply with laboratory safety and regulatory requirements collaborate effectively with multidisciplinary research teams and external partners About you a PhD in Polymer Chemistry, Chemistry, Materials Science or a closely related discipline demonstrated experience in polymer synthesis, characterisation and materials analysis a strong publication record relative to career stage in high-quality peer-reviewed journals experience using relevant analytical techniques such as NMR, SEC, DSC, DMA, rheology or related methods evidence of the ability to undertake independent research and contribute to collaborative projects strong written and verbal communication skills, including scientific publication and presentation experience demonstrated capacity to supervise or mentor students and junior researchers commitment to research integrity, laboratory safety and inclusive research practices Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
