    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Statistics_0157737-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Statistics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nOne 5-year and one 3-year full-time fixed term position, located on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Join a world-class community of statisticians, data scientists and mathematicians Base Salary, Academic Level B $132,533 – $157,381 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking a Lecturer in Statistics who will have the opportunity to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning design, delivery, and evaluation in statistics while continuing to build their own research program and network. These roles are replacements for staff members who are on fellowships.   Please specify the preferred duration of the contract in your cover letter.   Your key responsibilities will be to: teach graduate and undergraduate courses in statistics and data science for students from different disciplinary backgrounds conduct high-quality research in statistics or related areas supervise and coordinate graduate and undergraduate students contribute to the administration and collegial life of the School, working closely with colleagues in statistics and related areas attract external funding.   About you   a PhD (or near completion) in statistics or a closely related area evidence of independent research, compatible with the standards at The University of Sydney evidence of ability, versatility, and commitment to excellence in teaching statistics.   The following will be highly regarded:   5-year position: demonstrated ability to build productive collaborations with researchers from diverse disciplinary backgrounds and to translate statistical methodology into practice through consultation and collaborative research experience teaching statistics or data science to students from a range of disciplinary backgrounds evidence of research impact through collaborative publications, methodological adoption, or contributions to successful funding applications   3-year position: experience working with industry, government or other external partners a strong record in methodological or applied statistics, with demonstrated impact experience in teaching leadership and administration (e.g. unit coordination)   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/International-Development-and-Sponsorships-Manager_0157279-1": {
      "title": "International Development and Sponsorships Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Lead Global Partnerships and Grow International Sponsorship Opportunities HE0 9: $152,045 - $160,430 plus 17% Superannuation     About the opportunity     The International Development and Sponsorships Manager is a senior leadership role within Sydney Future Students' International team, responsible for driving the growth and success of the University's international sponsorship portfolio. Leading a small team, you will develop and strengthen relationships with government agencies, scholarship bodies, sponsors and international partners to increase sponsored student enrolments and support the University's internationalisation strategy.   Working at a strategic level, you will oversee sponsorship programs, including Australia Awards and other sponsored student cohorts, while acting as the University's key relationship manager for sponsors and government stakeholders. You will work closely with embassies, consulates, scholarship agencies and DFAT to identify new opportunities, strengthen existing partnerships and ensure the University remains a preferred education partner for sponsored students globally.   While your team manages the day-to-day administration and support of sponsored students, your focus will be on partnership development, business growth, stakeholder engagement and delivering long-term strategic outcomes. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced international education professional to influence institutional growth, represent the University on a global stage and build meaningful partnerships that create life-changing educational opportunities.       Key responsibilities   Develop and implement strategies to grow sponsored student enrolments and expand the University's international sponsorship portfolio. Build and maintain strategic relationships with sponsors, government agencies, embassies, consulates, scholarship bodies and other key international stakeholders. Lead, coach and develop a high-performing team, ensuring exceptional service delivery and operational excellence. Act as the University's primary liaison with sponsors and government stakeholders, including DFAT, on strategic sponsorship and partnership matters. Identify emerging market opportunities, deliver business development initiatives and provide strategic advice to support recruitment growth. Represent the University locally and internationally through stakeholder engagement activities, events and partnership initiatives.   About you   Demonstrated leadership experience managing and developing high-performing teams within international education, partnerships, business development or student recruitment environments. Proven success building and managing strategic relationships with government agencies, sponsors, scholarship providers or other complex external stakeholders. Strong business development experience with the ability to identify opportunities, influence outcomes and deliver sustainable growth. Experience engaging with senior stakeholders across diverse cultural environments, with highly developed communication, negotiation and relationship management skills. Strong understanding of the international higher education sector and the factors influencing international student recruitment and sponsorship programs. Relevant tertiary qualifications or equivalent experience, with the ability to travel domestically and internationally as required.   Join a globally recognised institution and play a pivotal role in strengthening international partnerships, growing sponsored student opportunities and enhancing the University's global impact.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow---Coastal-Geomorphology_0156265-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Coastal Geomorphology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time Fixed term for 3 years at Camperdown Join an exciting new research initiative in coastal wetland sedimentology Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The School of Geosciences is seeking a Research Fellow - Coastal Geomorphology to join a newly established research position. This role will contribute to a major research project focused on coastal wetland sedimentology, advancing understanding of sediment processes, landscape evolution and environmental change in coastal systems.   Working under broad direction, you will undertake high-quality independent and collaborative research, contribute to publications and research outputs, and participate in field-based investigations across coastal environments. The position offers an outstanding opportunity to build a strong research profile while collaborating with leading academics and external partners. Your key responsibilities will be to: undertake research in coastal wetland sedimentology and related geomorphological processes conduct fieldwork, sample collection and data analysis across coastal environments publish high-quality research outputs in leading scholarly journals contribute to conference presentations, seminars and research dissemination activities support the development of competitive research funding applications develop productive collaborations with academic, industry and government partners contribute to the supervision and mentoring of higher degree research students where appropriate maintain excellence in research quality, safety and project delivery contribute to a positive and inclusive research culture within the School. About you PhD in coastal geomorphology, sedimentology, Earth science, environmental science or a closely related discipline demonstrated experience conducting original research and developing a research profile in a relevant field expertise in coastal wetland processes, sediment dynamics and/or geomorphological research proven experience undertaking field-based research in coastal environments strong publication record relative to career stage excellent quantitative, analytical and scientific communication skills ability to work independently and collaboratively within multidisciplinary research teams developing network of academic, industry and professional stakeholders The School of Geosciences at the University of Sydney is a dynamic and collegial multi-disciplinary community of scholars and students encompassing the fields of geology and geophysics, marine science, environmental studies, and physical and human geography. It has a strong global reputation for the quality of its research and teaching across those domains. Our common passion is to engage in research and teaching that promotes a better understanding of our world and its challenges, and we address key issues facing society including climate change, resource management and sustainability.   The School of Geosciences is firmly committed to creating a diverse workplace by improving equity, access and opportunity. We want to create an environment where individuality is welcomed and celebrated, and a workplace that is open, supportive and safe for everyone. We are actively seeking to improve the diversity of our staffing profile, and welcome applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, and members of the LGBTIQ+ community.   To learn more about the School of Geosciences, click here Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School of Geosciences, Professor Kerrylee Rogers via email: kerrylee.rogers@sydney.edu.au   If you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Deepna Chand, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 29 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Digital-Projects-Specialist_0155994-2": {
      "title": "Digital Projects Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term position until November 2027 Play a pivotal role in the delivery of the Library’s digital services programs Base Salary $117,133 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Digital Projects Specialist provides project leadership, oversight and specialist advice to support the delivery of the Library’s digital projects and initiatives. In this role, you will develop and implement project plans, coordinate activities and deliverables, and monitor progress to ensure projects achieve agreed milestones and outcomes. You will work closely with stakeholders across the Library and University, managing project communications, building effective relationships and supporting collaboration across project teams. You will also contribute to identifying opportunities to enhance digital services and platforms through the effective use of technology. About you demonstrated project management experience, particularly delivering technology or digital projects within a complex organisation demonstrated ability to develop and manage project plans, activities, timelines and deliverables to achieve agreed outcomes demonstrated high-level interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to engage and influence a diverse range of stakeholders across complex projects demonstrated experience developing, implementing or managing digital services and platforms, preferably within a library or higher education environment demonstrated ability to identify and evaluate emerging technologies and digital solutions, and translate these into initiatives that support learning, teaching and research and enhance library services demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively, using initiative, sound judgement and problem-solving skills to manage issues and competing priorities Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Laura Pierce, Recruitment Operations by email to Laura.pierce@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 13 October 2026 11:59 PM"
