    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Daffodil-Centre-Research-Fellow---Senior-Research-Fellow_0157465": {
      "title": "Daffodil Centre Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 2-year fixed-term opportunities (with a possible 1-year extension) at the Daffodil Centre, based in Moore College (Camperdown campus) Join the Daffodil Centre as either a Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow, and use your expertise, research leadership and national profile to deliver high-quality cancer research with a population focus Academic Level B $132,533 - $157,381 or Academic Level C $162,347 - $187,198 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Daffodil Centre is a world-leading research centre, directly influencing policy decisions and real-world public health and clinical practice. The Centre is a partnership that combines the complementary strengths of the University of Sydney and Cancer Council NSW to accelerate progress in cancer prevention, early detection and cancer outcomes across the population. The partnership has created an unparalleled centre of national and international research excellence where research results in real-world improvements to reduce the burden from cancer in the community. Join the Daffodil Centre at the University of Sydney and contribute to research that is shaping the future of cancer control in Australia and internationally. Either as a Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow, you will bring recognised expertise and an established research profile to an environment where research excellence is translated into meaningful outcomes for policy, practice and communities.   Working with a high degree of autonomy and under broad direction, you will lead impactful research, pursue competitive funding opportunities, build strategic partnerships and provide expert advice within your area of expertise, ideally related to cancer prevention or early detection with a population focus. You will play an important role in strengthening the Centre’s research standing and influence, while mentoring emerging researchers and contributing to its broader strategic, academic and governance priorities. This is an opportunity to build on your national reputation for research excellence, expand your influence within your discipline and contribute to the Daffodil Centre’s mission to deliver research that makes a real difference to cancer control.   We would like to hear from early- or mid-career researchers who have completed a PhD related to public health, epidemiology, health services research, health economics, biostatistics, implementation science, behavioural science, health policy or clinical fields. Experience working with priority populations as defined in the Australian Cancer Plan will be highly regarded. Mentoring and career development opportunities will be provided.   A description of the Daffodil Centre and its teams is available on the Centre website . Have a preference for a certain research project and/or research team? Feel free to specify within your application when applying for the role.   Your key responsibilities as an Academic Level B Research Fellow:   Conduct high-quality original cancer research independently and collaboratively, contributing to publications, conference presentations and competitive research funding applications. Build and maintain a strong research profile through peer-reviewed publications, conference and seminar presentations, and other scholarly outputs. Develop research collaborations and contribute to translational research that informs policy and practice and delivers meaningful outcomes for the community, profession and government. Contribute to the development of competitive research grant applications and research activities that generate impact and support the strategic priorities of the Daffodil Centre and University. Provide training, informal mentoring and guidance to colleagues and postgraduate research students, where appropriate, while contributing to a positive, collaborative and inclusive research culture. Develop productive relationships with research partners, government, industry and other external stakeholders, contributing expertise to forums, seminars, conferences and public debate.   In addition to the above, as an Academic Level C Senior Research Fellow:   Establish and nurture partnerships and collaborations that support cancer research and deliver meaningful outcomes across policy, practice and research. Mentor and support early-career researchers, contributing to a collaborative culture of academic excellence, inclusion and innovation. Contribute to the Centre’s research, governance and strategic priorities, including academic committees, community engagement and activities that strengthen the impact and profile of the Daffodil Centre.   About you as an Academic Level B Research Fellow:   Completed PhD, with demonstrated expertise in cancer research methodologies, design and delivery. A developing research profile, with evidence of original research, scholarly publications, presentations and/or other research outputs relevant to your discipline or area of expertise. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively, applying sound judgement to research activities and providing informed advice and recommendations. A developing network of academic, government, and professional partners and stakeholders, with strong communication and relationship-building skills. Proven experience in planning and organising research activities, as well as supervising, mentoring or supporting colleagues and/or research students where appropriate.   In addition to the above, as an Academic Level C Senior Research Fellow:   A proven track record of research leadership, with the ability to develop influential research programs and contribute to expanding knowledge and practice. Significant experience supervising and mentoring researchers, with a commitment to supporting the development of emerging academic talent. An established network across academic, government, and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders, with the ability to leverage these relationships to strengthen research collaboration and impact.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. In your cover letter (no more than 2 pages), please outline your research area of interest and proposed research ideas for the Fellowship, and how it would fit with the Daffodil Centre.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Level C Senior Research Fellow Position Description . Click to view the Level B Research Fellow Position Description . Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Director--Australian-Centre-for-Robotics_0157383-1": {
      "title": "Director, Australian Centre for Robotics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nLead one of Australia’s premier robotics centres to shape the future of autonomous systems through world-class research, innovation, and strategic partnerships. Five-year fixed-term position with an attractive remuneration package, including the possibility of a substantive continuing academic appointment at Level D/E or Professor of Practice depending on candidate profile.   About the opportunity The Faculty of Engineering seeks to appoint an exceptional academic or industry leader as Director of the Australian Centre for Robotics (ACFR). The ACFR is a global leader in robotics, autonomous systems, and intelligent machines, recognised for its pioneering research and real-world impact across industry and government. Appointed at an appropriate senior academic level (Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor of Practice), the Director will provide strategic, academic, and operational leadership to ensure ACFR remains at the forefront of robotics research and innovation. They will lead the Centre’s strategic direction, research performance, financial sustainability, and engagement with industry, government, and international collaborators. Working closely with the Associate Dean (Research), the Dean, Heads of School, and senior University leaders, the Director will align ACFR’s strategy with institutional priorities in robotics, artificial intelligence, and intelligent systems. Externally, they will represent ACFR to national and global partners, strengthening its reputation as Australia’s leading robotics research hub. Exceptional leaders from academia, industry, government, or applied research organisations are encouraged to apply. The substantive academic appointment will be tailored to the successful candidate’s qualifications, experience, and career trajectory. The Director may also contribute to teaching, supervision, mentoring, or professional training in line with their academic level. About you (Skills, capabilities and experience) You will be an accomplished academic or industry leader with deep expertise in robotics or autonomous systems and a demonstrated record of excellence in research, translation, or major engineering achievements. Candidates without a higher education qualification but with exceptional professional achievements and recognised leadership in robotics or autonomous systems may be considered for appointment as Professor of Practice. The successful candidate will: hold a PhD or equivalent terminal qualification in robotics, autonomous systems, or a closely related field demonstrate excellence in robotics and autonomous systems through scholarly outputs, major engineering achievements, translational impact, or commercial innovation be recognised internationally as a leading authority in robotics or related domains, evidenced through significant achievements, honours, or leadership roles demonstrate a strong track record of industry engagement, including partnerships with government, industry, and professional associations possess strong planning and organisational capabilities, with the ability to deliver multiple concurrent strategic initiatives show excellent communication and stakeholder management skills, with the capacity to influence and inspire at senior levels have proven people leadership experience, including mentoring, performance management, and building high-performing teams foster a collaborative and inclusive culture, with a strong commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion contribute to teaching, supervision, mentoring, or professional training in line with the substantive academic appointment.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration This is a Full-time, Fixed term opportunity for 5 years . The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement  At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure for further details. Candidate information brochure can be downloaded via this link: https://filesender.aarnet.edu.au/?s=download&token=5a17b1c1-5847-4071-8820-7c9e876c6efb The University has engaged executive search firm The Insight Group to assist with this appointment. The Insight Group team will support the Selection Committee to identify the widest possible field of qualified candidates and to assess candidates against the requirements for the role. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact Rohan Carr or Emily Witts at The Insight Group, P: +61 3 9654 3288 or email: rohancarr@insightgroup.com.au , emilywitts@insightgroup.com.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Wednesday 28 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Administration-Officer_0156484-2": {
      "title": "Senior Administration Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nFull-time fixed-term opportunity from January 2027 to June 2027 HEO Level 6, Base Salary starting from $107,312 p.a. + 17% superannuation Suitable as a fixed-term appointment or secondment opportunity for internal candidates Join the Sydney Pharmacy School and play a key role in supporting operational and academic administration activities About the opportunity   Sydney Pharmacy School is internationally recognised for excellence in education and research and is committed to delivering outstanding support services that enable academic and professional staff to thrive. The school is seeking an experienced and highly organised Senior Administration Officer to join our professional services team on a fixed-term basis from January 2027 to June 2027 to cover a period of staff leave. This opportunity is also available as a secondment for eligible internal candidates Working closely with the School Manager, the Senior Administration Officer is a key member of the Executive Support team and provides high-level administrative, operational and project support across a broad range of school activities. This role serves as a central point of coordination for administration, human resources, finance, facilities, events and stakeholder engagement activities, helping to ensure the efficient and effective operation of the School. The successful candidate will provide expert administrative support, manage complex enquiries and operational matters, contribute to continuous improvement initiatives and maintain strong relationships across the Faculty, University and external stakeholders. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced administrator who enjoys variety, problem-solving and working in a collaborative and customer-focused environment. Key responsibilities Provide high-level administrative and operational support to the school manager and executive support team manage a broad range of administrative activities, ensuring efficient and professional service delivery to staff, students and stakeholders respond to complex enquiries and administrative matters, exercising sound judgement, discretion and initiative provide advice and guidance on university policies, procedures and administrative processes support school staffing and human resource administration activities, including assisting with hr processes, leave management and policy enquiries coordinate meetings, events, workshops and conferences, including scheduling, logistics, travel arrangements, agendas and minutes manage correspondence and ensure matters are appropriately prioritised, actioned and escalated where required support financial administration activities including invoice processing, purchasing, budget tracking, travel acquittals and reporting prepare reports, data summaries, presentations and documentation to support decision-making and operational planning maintain accurate records and ensure compliance with university policies and procedures build and maintain productive relationships with academic and professional staff across the faculty and university identify opportunities for process improvement and contribute to initiatives that enhance operational effectiveness collaborate with administrative colleagues across the faculty and contribute to a culture of continuous improvement and service excellence undertake other duties commensurate with the classification level of the position.   About you Relevant tertiary qualifications in business administration or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of training and experience demonstrated administrative experience within a large and complex organisation, preferably within the higher education sector experience providing high-level administrative and executive support in a fast-paced environment well-developed organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines strong problem-solving skills and the ability to exercise sound judgement and initiative excellent verbal and written communication skills and a commitment to delivering high-quality customer service demonstrated ability to build effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders experience supporting financial administration processes, including purchasing, invoice management, expenditure monitoring and reporting strong attention to detail and commitment to accuracy. advanced computer skills, including proficiency in Microsoft office and administrative systems experience working collaboratively within a team environment and contributing positively to workplace culture a commitment to fostering inclusive, respectful and professional working environments. To keep our community safe, please be aware of our COVID safety precautions which form our conditions of entry for all staff, students and visitors coming to campus. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rachel Yazigi Recruitment Operations by email to rachel.yazigi@sydney.edu.au.   Applications should include: A cover letter addressing your suitability for the role A current resume/CV   For employees of the University of Sydney, internal applicants interested in a secondment opportunity are encouraged to discuss their application with their current manager prior to applying. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Manager--Indigenous-Employment-and-Engagement--Identified-_0157675-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Indigenous Employment and Engagement (Identified)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Drive Indigenous employment and engagement initiatives, fostering an inclusive, culturally safe workplace and supporting the attraction, retention and development of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   This position has been designated for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people only under the provisions of section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW). Should your application be progressed, applicants will be required to provide confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity in line with university requirements.   About the opportunity   Lead the delivery of Indigenous employment and engagement strategies that strengthen attraction, retention, career development and wellbeing for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, while fostering a culturally safe, inclusive and supportive workplace across the University.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead the development and delivery of Indigenous employment and engagement programs, initiatives and staff networks that support a culturally safe, inclusive and welcoming workplace drive Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander recruitment, retention, career development and leadership pathways, partnering with stakeholders across the university to achieve workforce goals develop, monitor and report on key workforce metrics, targets and strategic objectives, using data and insights to inform decision-making and measure progress provide expert advice on Indigenous employment, cultural safety, diversity and inclusion, influencing policies, practices and organisational strategies build strong partnerships with internal and external stakeholders to promote best practice, enhance capability and support the successful delivery of Indigenous workforce initiatives lead and develop a high-performing team while managing key projects, budgets, governance reporting and strategic initiatives aligned with the university's Indigenous workforce plan   About you   tertiary or postgraduate qualifications in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of training and experience extensive experience delivering diversity, inclusion and Indigenous employment outcomes within complex organisations, with a strong understanding of strategic hr and industrial relations frameworks demonstrated expertise in Indigenous employment, cultural competence, and the attraction, retention and development of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and other diverse workforce groups proven ability to build and influence relationships with a broad range of stakeholders, using strong communication, consultation and relationship management skills to deliver strategic outcomes strong leadership, strategic planning and project management capability, with experience managing multiple complex initiatives, budgets, contractors and competing priorities highly developed analytical, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to use workforce data, research and insights to inform recommendations, drive cultural change and achieve measurable results   Why work with us We know our people are what make the University's mission possible. Alongside meaningful work, we offer a range of benefits designed to support you financially, personally and professionally. Key benefits include: Paid Cultural and Ceremonial Leave: Indigenous employees are entitled to 5 days of paid leave and 10 days of unpaid leave each year to participate in cultural and ceremonial activities and fulfil community obligations Career Development : Access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding Community and Connection: Be part of a vibrant community with opportunities to connect through staff networks (our YURA Network), mentoring, cultural events and professional Development opportunities Flexible Work and Exceptional Benefits: Enjoy flexible work arrangements, 17% employer superannuation, Fitness Passport, generous leave provisions, staff discounts and access to a wide range of University facilities and services.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  This is an identified opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Applicants will be asked to provide confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander identity as part of the application process. We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers by email to  victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Associate-Director--Faculty-Partnerships_0157595-2": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing position as an Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships Key leadership position supporting Sydney Future Students   SPS A – Competitive remuneration package plus 17% superannuation   About the opportunity     The Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships , is a key operational leadership role responsible for supporting Sydney Future Students in developing and implementing faculty- and school-level recruitment strategies and services for the University of Sydney. This includes establishing, implementing, and reviewing evidence-based approaches to resource planning, priority setting, and decision-making across the Faculty Partnerships function, and using data, market intelligence, insights and other relevant information to develop recruitment plans to support university and faculty level priorities in collaboration with the Sydney Future Students leadership team.      The role of Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships has been designed to lead the team in providing a highly professional, specialised, and effective student recruitment service through activities and strategic partnerships. This is an excellent opportunity to bring your extensive higher education, operational expertise, service model, people leadership and exceptional stakeholder management skills to the forefront of your role. This role is one where your initiative, creativity, and leadership experience will help to deliver excellence to our stakeholders and support the Sydney Future Students portfolio in delivering best-in-market student recruitment expertise and product insights.     The Associate Director, Faculty Partnerships will work under the broad direction of the Director, Domestic Recruitment and Access, and will work collaboratively with stakeholders to continuously improve the Faculty Partnerships offering on behalf of Sydney Future Students. The Associate Director will apply significant knowledge and experience to focus on continuous improvement approaches to support the delivery of highly professional student recruitment outcomes in line with the broader University and faculty/schools’ strategic goals. This role is pivotal in shaping the team's service offering and representing the team at senior levels across the institution.        Your key responsibilities will be to:       lead the team’s key strategic planning activities, including the development of strategic, operational, and business plans aligned with university-wide targets and faculty priorities  lead and provide high-level analysis and information in support of the achievement of SFS’s strategic goals  work with the leadership team to develop and deliver high-quality reports on market and student demand trends, intake scenarios, profiles, course and field of education, and subject-specific rankings and changes, and competitors’ activities. ensure the Faculty Partnerships team has a clear understanding of faculty and school-level objectives and a means to provide regular updates on student recruitment activities to the Associate Vice-President, SFS leadership, Deans, faculty and school staff and faculty/school Marketing and Communications teams    support university product development, review and market analysis projects, contributing to university-wide and faculty curriculum projects, engaging and coordinating input from other members of the Sydney Future Students leadership team manage the allocation of resources, ensuring alignment with the strategic direction of student recruitment, including developing and monitoring the allocated faculty/school-level budgets within Faculty Partnerships facilitate evidence-based decision-making by supporting the development of data, product, market, and student recruitment reporting literacy for the Faculty Partnerships team as required build and foster a culture of innovation, creativity, excellence, collaboration and accountability.         About you      substantial understanding of university faculty/school-based needs in managing their student recruitment objectives   high-level planning and organisational skills, and the ability to coordinate multiple streams of stakeholders   highly developed leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills, including skills in large-scale, complex team and stakeholder management and internal relationship building, negotiation, influencing and motivation, and the ability to prioritise and effectively manage team workloads   superior relationship management skills and capacity to manage competing needs of diverse stakeholders, with various time and resource limitations previous experience in higher education leadership, and working knowledge of university, faculty, and school-level planning, reporting and governance.       Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Brownlow-Hill/Veterinary-Specialist-in-Equine-Surgery_0157518-1": {
      "title": "Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nBe part of building the future of veterinary clinical education at the University of Sydney   Full time three year fixed term opportunity Located on Camden Campus   Opportunity to inspire and train the next generation of veterinarians, engage with the school ranked # 1 in Australia, within a world-class university Base Salary HEO 9 (38 hours), $165,080 - $174,177 p.a. + 17% superannuation + specialist loading when registered in NSW as a specialist to a total package of up to $234,737 p.a     About the opportunity   Your chance to have a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession as you care for the lives of animals.   We are seeking to appoint a Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery to join a team of outstanding world-class clinical colleagues at the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Camden.   We support and encourage excellence in all aspects of the role, from patient care, teaching and learning, and research. The school values highly the tenants of mutual support, respect, empathy, collegiality and integrity.   Join an organization that has over a hundred years of experience in clinical training and innovation. Be part of the transformational process supporting students as they transition to become veterinarians. A privileged role for someone who wants more than a job- come and do work with a purpose.   Your key responsibilities will be to   maintain reputation for academic excellence provide high-quality specialist equine surgical services for clients of the UVTHC during business hours develop and support an expanding client base and equine caseload for the hospital participate in the provision of education to DVM students and continuing veterinary education activities provided by the unit coordinate the daily activities of the equine unit and provide additional support as required contribute to the continuous improvement of clinical standards and practice provide support to the Camden Equine Hospital after-hours service on a rotating basis actively participate in meetings, planning and development activities within the UVTHC, as required by the Hospital Director and Hospital Manager.   To learn more about the Sydney School of Veterinary Science click here   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery :   Veterinary Specialist in Equine Surgery (HEO level 9) who is a veterinarian with at least partial responsibility for the management of a unit or division, including the management of a range of staff working within the relevant discipline or sub-discipline, who has:   ·       a degree in veterinary science registrable in New South Wales, Australia ·       a Diplomate or Fellowship permitting registration with the NSW Veterinary Scientists Board as a Specialist in Equine / Large Animal Surgery.   The commencement level will be determined based on the candidate’s skills, experience and knowledge. The 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board.   The key benefits   opportunity to learn and enhance your skills and experience in the unique teaching and research environment of a Veterinary Teaching hospital salary packaging available 17% of your base salary, which significantly exceeds the standard Australian superannuation guarantee rate 15% veterinary clinical specialist loading is available once registered as specialist by NSW Veterinary Practitioners Board industry-leading leave provisions including generous paid parental leave up to 38 weeks entitlements (pending eligibility) compressed work week available pending eligiblity generous relocation allowance when moving interstate or internationally access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres staff discounts on selected bank products and health insurance gain the recognition of working in a leading Veterinary School in an outstanding University.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Visa sponsorship is available   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all the role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   If you are a current employee of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Chris Masaoka, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources by email recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Multiple-Continuing--Tenure-Track--Academic-Positions---Faculty-of-Engineering--The-University-of-Sydney_0157992-1": {
      "title": "Multiple Continuing (Tenure-Track) Academic Positions,  Faculty of Engineering, The University of Sydney",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nJoin a thriving Faculty of Engineering at a University ranked amongst the world’s best teaching and research institutions and help shape the future of artificial intelligence and its impact across every field.  Located in the heart of Sydney’s bustling inner west, close to beaches, parks, public transport and shopping districts.  We are seeking outstanding academics at Level B to D who want to be part of how AI is reshaping the world, whether by leveraging AI to advance their field, seeking to understand how AI works, or building the technologies that make it possible.  About the opportunity The Faculty of Engineering at The University of Sydney (Sydney, Australia) invites applications for several academic positions at Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, and Associate Professor (Level B-D) across our Engineering schools. We are seeking academics whose research engages with AI including, but not limited to: LEVERAGE AI: Use AI to advance research, discovery, engineering practice, design, computation, education and other fields — applying AI to new problems and unlocking new possibilities. UNDERSTAND AI: Investigate how AI works, from its mathematical, computational and engineering foundations to its capabilities, limitations, behaviour, impacts and implications. BUILD AI: Develop the next generation of AI methods, models, systems, tools and technologies, and the engineering and computational foundations that enable them. We welcome researchers whose connection to AI is established today, as well as those who can articulate how AI will shape their current or future research.  Our Engineering Schools School of Aerospace, Mechanical and Mechatronic Engineering   School of Biomedical Engineering    School of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering     School of Civil Engineering     School of Computer Science   School of Electrical and Computer Engineering   School of Project Management   All positions are full-time, continuing (tenure-track), teaching and research roles based in Sydney.  Successful applicants will join a group of enthusiastic, highly research-active and collegiate academics who deliver a world-class education to our undergraduate and postgraduate students. Successful applicants will have an excellent research track record, a commitment to high-quality teaching with the ability to teach in a range of courses, and a vision for how AI can advance research, education and practice across their field. The role offers strong opportunities for collaboration with world-class researchers and University key partners, including the Sydney Biomedical Accelerator , as well as opportunities to develop new interdisciplinary collaborations across the University. About you The University values trust, accountability and excellence . As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values. You will develop an independent research program and engage in collaborations within and outside the University, including with industry partners. You will contribute to curriculum development and delivery at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels and contribute to leadership of the School/Faculty. You will be able to demonstrate:  a PhD in a relevant field. Applicants who have submitted their PhD for examination or will do so in the immediate future can be considered a strong track record in research appropriate to the discipline and level of appointment (relative to opportunity and taking career disruptions into account)  ability to teach, engage, and support the learning of students using innovative and modern pedagogies, including the organisation and delivery of a unit of study, with an eye to how AI is reshaping teaching and learning in your field outstanding communication skills and collegiality  high-level problem-solving skills with the ability to address complex issues as they arise. The Faculty of Engineering is committed to increasing the gender equity and diversity of its academic staff and strongly encourages women and candidates from underrepresented groups in Engineering to apply. How to apply Candidates should submit a CV, a research statement (up to 2 pages), and a teaching statement (up to 2 pages). We recognise that individual circumstances can impact career opportunity and trajectory and encourage the inclusion of a statement outlining any career disruptions or relative to opportunity information in the candidate CV. Please include the names and email addresses of three referees. Current employees of the University or a contingent worker with access to Workday, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Please note while all applications are welcome, individual Schools may prioritise candidates aligned with specific focus areas and, with the candidate’s permission, redirect applications to other Schools where there is stronger alignment. Term and Remuneration The level of appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Successful applicants should expect an internationally competitive salary, a generous start-up package and first-class research, learning and teaching environment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. About us The University of Sydney is Australia’s first university with an outstanding global reputation for academic and research excellence. Since our inception 175 years ago, The University of Sydney has led to improve the world around us. We believe in education for all, and that effective leadership in education and research makes lives better. These same values are reflected in our approach to diversity and inclusion and underpin our long-term strategy for growth. Across our campuses, we employ over 7600 academic and non-academic staff who support over 70,000 students.  We are undergoing significant transformative change which brings opportunity for innovation, progressive thinking, breaking with convention, challenging the status quo, and improving the world around us. As part of its Strategic Plan 2025–2028 the Faculty of Engineering is committed to driving world-leading research with global impact, cultivating future-ready engineers, delivering innovative, student-centred education, and fostering a diverse, supportive, and collaborative community.  The University of Sydney is recognised as one of the top research and teaching universities in the world. We are a globally ranked university , ranked 28th in QS University World Rankings 2027, 3rd for sustainability in Australia.  EEO statement At The University of Sydney, we are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds, including women. We are proud to be recognised with a Silver Athena Swan Award and as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  Anticipated timelines Please refer to the Please refer to the Engineering Recruitment Guidelines for key information about this recruitment campaign. Contact Information For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant. Recruitment Operations, Human Resources on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email hr.engineeringcampaign@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Descriptions:   Click to view the  Position Description for Lecturer – Level B role. Click to view the  Position Description for Senior Lecturer - Level C role. Click to view the  Position Description for Associate Professor – Level D role. Applications Close Monday 30 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Applied-Mathematics_0157732-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Applied Mathematics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time 4 years fixed term position, l ocated on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Opportunity to make significant contributions to teaching and learning practice, design and evaluation while continuing to build research expertise Base Salary, Academic Level B $132,533 – $157,381 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity The School of Mathematics and Statistics is currently seeking a Lecturer in Applied Mathematics who will have the opportunity to make a significant contribution to teaching and learning design, delivery, and evaluation in pure mathematics while continuing to build research contribution, expertise, connections, and impact. This role is a replacement for a staff member who is on a fellowship. Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with other School members in applied mathematics, or related areas conduct high-quality research in applied mathematics, or related areas teach graduate and undergraduate courses in mathematics for students from different disciplinary backgrounds supervise and coordinate graduate and undergraduate students apply for external funding. About you a PhD (or near completion) in mathematics, applied mathematics, or a related area evidence of independent research, compatible with the standards at The University of Sydney evidence of ability, versatility, and commitment to excellence in teaching. Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Mathematics_0157704-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Mathematics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time 4 years fixed term position, l ocated on the Camperdown Campus at the School of Mathematics and Statistics Opportunity to join a vibrant and collegial community of world-class mathematicians and statisticians Base Salary, Academic Level B $132,533 – $157,381 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   We are one of the largest mathematical sciences schools in Australia, and the University of Sydney is the only Australian university to have received the highest rating of 5 out of 5 for research in the mathematical sciences in every Australian Research Council Excellence in Research for Australia assessment to date. The University also hosts the Sydney Mathematical Research Institute, which attracts leading mathematical scientists from all over the world to do research with Australian collaborators. We are currently seeking to appoint an accomplished academic to the position of Lecturer in Mathematics . To succeed, you will be a talented and well-qualified mathematician with a passion for teaching and research expertise in an area that strengthens the school’s profile. This includes, but is not limited to Geometric Analysis, Differential Geometry, and Nonlinear Partial Differential Equations . You will need to have appropriate skills and qualifications as outlined in the position description, including a PhD in Mathematics. If appointed, you will be expected to undertake duties consistent with an appointment at the Lecturer level (Level B) on the Australian academic scale. This will include: publishing scholarly papers in international peer-reviewed journals and presenting at conferences; supervising undergraduate and postgraduate students; teaching a range of courses to undergraduate students; and applying for external funding.   This role is a replacement for a staff member who is on a fellowship.   About you   a PhD in Mathematics or related areas demonstrated evidence of independent research ability and potential in an area of Mathematics published research, as a sole author or in collaboration, in refereed journals evidence of ability, versatility and commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching ability to supervise students at undergraduate and postgraduate levels demonstrated capacity to work co-operatively with others evidence of administration and course coordination within a university environment and the ability to apply for competitive research funding would be desirable.   To apply for this role, please submit the below three documents in addition to your CV, in support of your application: cover letter outlining your suitability for this position research statement, with a focus on where you see your research in 4 years teaching statement, with a focus on approaches you utilize for effective teaching.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow_0157700-2": {
      "title": "Research Fellow-Sydney Nursing School",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSeeking talented Health Services Researchers 2 x Full-time, fixed term (until 30/01/2029), Research Fellow positions with flexibility to consider part-time arrangements that can support both research needs and personal commitments Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 (pro rata for 0.80 FTE) + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   At the University of Sydney Susan Wakil School of Nursing and Midwifery (Sydney Nursing School) we integrate teaching, research, and scholarship with practice and policy development, while fostering engagement with all health professions.   We are seeking two Research Fellows (Health Services Researchers) to support Professor Melissa Baysari in a programme of research funded by an NHMRC Investigator Grant. The research will focus on safety of the new Electronic Health Record (EHR) system being rolled out across hospitals in NSW.   As a Research Fellow , you will play a key role in establishing and delivering this research program, working closely with healthcare services, policymakers and multidisciplinary researchers. Your work will focus on evaluating the implementation of the EHR with a particular emphasis on patient and staff safety, safe transition to a new system, management of system upgrades and downtime events, clinical decision support, and the broader impact of this technology on healthcare delivery.   This is a research-focused position providing an exciting opportunity to contribute to one of Australia's most significant health system transformation programs. We welcome applications from Health Services Researchers with experience or a strong interest in patient safety, digital health, human factors, health informatics, healthcare systems, or related disciplines. Research experience relating to electronic health records, digital systems, or large-scale healthcare change initiatives will be highly regarded.    Your key responsibilities will be to: contribute to establishing and delivering a major NHMRC Investigator Grant-funded program of research evaluate the impact of EHR implementation on patients, staff and quality of care investigate factors supporting safe transition from one EHR to another examine issues relating to user training, workforce wellbeing, system usability and downtime management engage with health services, government agencies and other stakeholders to support evidence-informed implementation and policy decisions publish findings in high-impact journals and present outcomes at national and international conferences contribute to grant applications, research translation activities and the development of sustainable research partnerships   About you a PhD in health services research, human factors, health informatics, public health, psychology, implementation science, nursing, pharmacy, or a related discipline demonstrated experience conducting independent and collaborative research with high-quality scholarly outputs experience or a strong interest in the evaluation of health technologies knowledge of, or experience working with, electronic health records or other digital health technologies is highly desirable strong quantitative, qualitative or mixed-methods research skills demonstrated ability to work effectively with healthcare organisations, policymakers and multidisciplinary teams excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to translate research findings into practice and policy Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Cecilia Blamey, Recruitment Operations by email to cecilia.blamey@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 15 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Manager-Insights_0156790-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager Insights",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time continuing opportunity Lead a high-performing team of Insights Analysts delivering strategic market research and data-driven insights SPS A - Competitive remuneration package plus 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   As the Senior Manager Insights , you will lead a team of Insights Analysts to deliver high-impact market research, analytics and actionable insights that support outstanding student experiences, informed decision-making and organisational performance across the University.   Partnering with stakeholders across faculties and portfolios, you will identify opportunities to apply market research and analytics to complex challenges, translate data into meaningful recommendations, and build a strong culture of evidence-based decision-making.   You will play a key leadership role, developing team capability, fostering strong stakeholder relationships, driving innovation in research and analytics practices, and ensuring the delivery of high-quality, impactful insights.     Key responsibilities   Lead and develop a team of Insights Analysts to deliver high-quality market research and actionable insights. Build strong stakeholder relationships and provide trusted advice to support strategic decision-making. Oversee the delivery of market research, analytics and insights projects from design through to implementation. Drive a culture of innovation, data-driven decision-making and continuous improvement. Ensure high standards of data quality, governance and operational excellence.   About you   Proven experience leading and developing high-performing teams in a complex environment. Extensive experience delivering market research, analytics and insights projects that drive business outcomes. Strong stakeholder management skills with the ability to build trusted relationships and influence at senior levels. Demonstrated ability to translate complex data into clear, actionable insights and recommendations for executive audiences. Expertise in market research methodologies and analytical tools, including platforms such as Qualtrics and Power BI. Excellent communication, project management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and deliver results.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or support filling out this application, please contact Kristy Fairweather, Recruitment Operations, by email to kristy.fairweather@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Admissions-Officer_0156242-1": {
      "title": "Admissions Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term position until August 2027 Excellent opportunity to represent and be a part of the University of Sydney’s Admissions Team Base Salary $93K + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity Working within the Admissions Office, the Admissions Officer works with the Admissions Manager and the Admissions Team Leader to ensure the effective, accurate and timely recruitment and admission of students to the University. The incumbent will use their experience, knowledge and expertise to implement business process improvements within admissions.   As an Admissions Officer you will update application records, databases and ensure admissions decisions comply with established procedures, the relevant University polices and guidelines.   If you are looking for an exciting opportunity to take your Administration career to the next level and join the higher education sector, apply now!   About you have at least 1-year working experience in higher education Admissions demonstrated experience in administrative competencies and high-level office administration, ideally within the admissions/Higher Education sector (but not essential) or other areas such as in data entry companies and other industries that require the use of various database systems highly organised and have the ability to work autonomously whilst also working well within a team delivers high volume, daily administration activities and services ensuring that high quality and efficient administrative support is provided in a pro-active manner to staff, students, and agents ability to exercise judgement when interpretating policy maintains professional, productive, and positive relationships with faculty administrative staff, academics and other internal stakeholders to enable effective delivery of services and issues resolution experience or understanding of the UAC admission process would be highly desirable have Microsoft Excel Skills and the ability to produce and interpret reports.   Please note: This role has a scheduled leave blackout period during December and January, during which annual leave cannot be taken.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son, Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au.   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
