    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-TESOL_0157493": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in TESOL",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing role based at the Camperdown Campus in the Sydney School of Education and Social Work Opportunity to lead and innovate high quality TESOL education at a leading global university Base Salary Level C $162,347 p.a. - $187,198 p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   Join the Sydney School of Education and Social Work within the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, the Senior Lecturer in TESOL role plays a key part in delivering high quality Teaching English to Speakers of Other Languages (TESOL), focuses on the teaching and learning of languages and literacy in both English-speaking and non-English-speaking nations.   You'll dive into the learning and teaching of English, languages other than English and English as a second or additional language/dialect (EAL/D). You’ll understand the implications of pedagogy, curriculum design and assessment and evaluation in schools, community organisations and higher education institutions.   Reporting to the Head of School, your key responsibilities will be:   coordinate and teach units related to TESOL and EAL/D curriculum in initial teacher education programs across primary and secondary education support ongoing education and research programs in the area of Community Languages education build and maintain reputation of academic excellence make original and innovative contribution to research and education which expand knowledge or practice maintain a research profile which meets University expectations and standards lead the development in research which enhance the reputation of the School, such as leading participation in internationally-funded research projects or partner-engaged research projects contribute to outstanding teaching and learning outcomes actively contribute to research, education and administrative activities contribute to collaborative teaching in the School and work flexibly and collaboratively in a team environment actively participate in governance and academic community at School and Faculty levels.     About you   We are seeking an exceptional Senior Lecturer in TESOL who has:   a PhD in the area of EAL/D or languages education or a related field experience in teaching and learning EAL/D and/or languages in primary and/or secondary and tertiary contexts demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives in the field of EAL/D and/or languages developing network of relationships with key academic, community, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience in supervising or mentoring others and/or coordinating team activities and research a developing knowledge and understanding of the key strategic issues in the EAL/D and languages discipline and higher education sector a developing network of academic, community, industry and professional partners and stakeholders.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Assistant---Dementia_0156621": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Dementia",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPart-time (14 hours per week) one-year fixed term position. Located at the Brain and Mind Centre on the Camperdown campus Join a high-performing research team and contribute to impactful research that advances knowledge and improves outcomes Base Salary $93,345 - $104,515 (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The School of Psychology are currently seeking a Research Assistant - Dementia to provide technical and operational support to the Chief Investigator and the team to support the research projects investigating cognitive changes in frontotemporal dementia and associated disorders.   Working closely with Prof.Olivier Piguet , the successful incumbent will provide technical and operational support to the Chief Investigator and the team of FRONTIER , the frontotemporal dementia clinical research group, to support research projects investigating frontotemporal dementia and associated disorders.   This position involves coordinating multidisciplinary research assessments, carrying out cognitive testing of study participants, participating in relevant meetings and contributing to administrative work in the area. The role may also require home visits at times.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   undertake clinical and cognitive assessment of people diagnosed with dementia (e.g., frontotemporal dementia, Alzheimer 's disease) or healthy older adults collect research data, enters/stores in appropriate statistical software, and prepares statistical analysis/reports as required ensure that the appropriate ethics applications are lodged in collaboration with the Chief Investigator, or Research Manager and/or Senior Academics coordinate the preparation of assessment protocol kits, scheduling of participants, research and prepare drafts for research publications and presentations including preparing literature reviews and carrying out theoretical research to support the overall research objectives.   About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a Research Assistant (Psychology ) who has:   an Honours degree – in Clinical Neuroscience, Psychology or relevant field prior experience working with older people, particularly people with dementia knowledge in the cognitive neuroscience of neurodegenerative disorders demonstrated experience in carrying out literary searches, and preparing literary reviews for further analysis proven experience in developing and working on research projects, including expertise in using research protocols, experimental design, data manipulation and analysis, ethics submissions and grant applications strong interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills with sound negotiating and conflict resolution skills and a demonstrated level of tact and discretion a current and valid Australian drivers license (desirable).   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Rebecca Astar or Cherie Goodwin, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Friday 16 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Senior-Manager--Risk-and-Resilience_0156533-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Risk and Resilience",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term opportunity until 28th Jan 2028   Provide strategic leadership in strengthening the University’s enterprise risk and resilience landscape    Offering a competitive salary package including 17% superannuation       About the opportunity     The University of Sydney is seeking an experienced and highly motivated Senior Manager, Risk and Resilience to join our Risk Management team.    This is an exciting opportunity to lead and shape the University's organisational resilience, crisis management and business continuity capability at one of Australia's leading universities.    Working closely with senior leaders across the University, you will play a key role in helping the organisation anticipate, prepare for, respond to and recover from disruption. You will lead initiatives that strengthen business continuity, support critical incident and crisis management arrangements, design and deliver resilience exercises, and embed practical resilience capability across faculties, schools and portfolios.    No two days are the same. One day you may be facilitating a crisis exercise with senior executives, the next supporting an incident response, reviewing business continuity plans, preparing advice for governance committees or helping stakeholders navigate a complex emerging issue before it escalates.    You will also play an important role in identifying emerging risks, recognising patterns and themes across incidents, business continuity activities and operational issues, and translating local insights into strategic advice for senior leaders. By connecting what is happening across the University, you will help ensure significant issues, resilience vulnerabilities and emerging threats are appropriately escalated, understood and considered within the University's enterprise risk framework and governance processes.    Working across a broad network of stakeholders, you will act as a trusted adviser on organisational resilience, preparedness, crisis management and emerging risk issues, helping leaders make informed decisions in complex and often time-sensitive situations.    As part of a highly regarded and collaborative Risk Management team, you will have the opportunity to influence enterprise-wide decision-making, contribute to meaningful organisational change and help strengthen the University's ability to navigate uncertainty, disruption and emerging challenges.      Your key responsibilities will be to:     provide strategic leadership for the University’s organisational resilience arrangements, including the Organisational Resilience Framework, Crisis Management Plan, business continuity approach, scenario exercises and post-incident review requirements  lead business continuity and preparedness uplift across the University, including Faculty, School and portfolio Business Continuity Plans (BCPs), Crown Jewel business continuity planning, testing, monitoring and continuous improvement  coordinate and support the University’s critical incident and crisis management arrangements, including Critical Incident Response Teams and Crisis Management Committee preparedness, activation support, escalation pathways, action and decision records, stand-down arrangements and post-incident review activities  design, facilitate and evaluate crisis simulations, scenario exercises, preparedness activities and post-incident reviews to strengthen organisational capability and lessons learned  support emerging risk and cross functional preparedness groups by providing risk and resilience advice on complex issues before they escalate into formal critical incidents or crises  identify systemic risks, resilience gaps and themes from incidents, exercises, business continuity activity, emerging risks, and risk registers, and translate these into practical recommendations for senior leaders and governance forums  lead the integration of resilience insights into the University’s Risk Management Framework, including risk appetite, risk profiles, control effectiveness, governance reporting and enterprise-level risk advice  deliver high-quality risk analysis, briefings and reports to support senior leadership and governance decision-making on enterprise risk, organisational resilience, preparedness and emerging issues  coordinate and guide the broader risk and resilience community, including risk owners, BCP coordinators and key stakeholders, to support consistent practice and continuous improvement.  act as a trusted adviser during incidents and periods of disruption, helping leaders make informed decisions in complex and time-sensitive situations.       About you     postgraduate qualifications In a relevant field or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience  excellent at interpreting data with highly developed problem solving and analytical techniques and the ability to translate complex data into high quality, succinct reports  extensive experience in risk management frameworks, including best practices, methodologies, and tools  extensive experience in strategic planning, analysis, decision-making support and advisory services  demonstrated professional experience and expertise in the application of risk identification and assessment and compliance and assurance techniques in an organisation operating in highly complex industry and environment  demonstrated adaptability and resourcefulness when meeting complex and conflicting demands, solving non-routine problems and priorities  superior written and oral communication and interpersonal skills including high level report writing, presentation and facilitation skills; understanding of a range of communication modes, including their suitability for different target audiences.   an interest in helping organisations prepare for uncertainty, strengthen resilience and continuously improve how they respond to disruption.      Sponsorship / work rights for Australia     You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.      Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.      EEO statement       At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.      We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .        How to apply     Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.      For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au .     © The University of Sydney    The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 18 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Deputy-Dean--Faculty-of-Arts-and-Social-Sciences_0156501-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Dean, Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nLocated in Camperdown 5 year FIXED-TERM position with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences (FASS) at the University of Sydney has commenced an international search for the key leadership role of Deputy Dean. The new Deputy Dean will be joining at a key time, as the University enters the second phase of its ten-year strategic cycle (Sydney in 2032) and the Faculty progresses its own strategic plan. The new Deputy Dean will work alongside the Dean, Professor Lisa Adkins, as FASS looks to consolidate its position as a world-leading centre for education and research across the humanities, arts, and social science disciplines. The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at Sydney is the oldest and one of the most comprehensive of its kind in Australia. The Faculty remains proud of its past while focused on the future. The Faculty’s distinctiveness lies in the depth and breadth of its disciplines, with its many research and teaching strengths earning it a deserved reputation for excellence nationally and internationally. The Faculty’s research is driven by the pursuit of multidisciplinary knowledge that benefits Australia and the wider world. In 2025 we welcomed two new flagship centres, the Centre for AI, Trust and Governance, which advances responsible AI development through multidisciplinary research, fosters public trust, and informs policy decisions in complex regulatory environments; and the Contemporary Inequalities Centre, which brings together multidisciplinary expertise to pursue collaborative, problem-driven research, aiming to respond to emerging social and economic challenges to build the evidence base needed to shape new policy responses. Our other flagship research centres include the Vere Gordon Childe Centre, and the Sydney Centre for Healthy Societies. Other research centres, institutes, and groups in the Faculty include the Sydney Institute for Community Languages Education, the CREATE Centre, and the Sydney Social Sciences and Humanities Advanced Research Centre (SSSHARC). For our students, and in collaboration with our partners, the Faculty offers internships and work placements, overseas exchange and interdisciplinary collaboration around complex, real-world problems. As we mature our connections with industry, community and government, the Faculty is seeking to establish anchoring partnerships operating across its research and education portfolios.   About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The Deputy Dean is a key member of the FASS leadership team, providing academic leadership, and contributing to strategy, operations, financial management, people management and internal and external stakeholder engagement, as well as deputising for the Dean (as required). A key responsibility of this role is strategically guiding and advising Disciplines and Schools to ensure effective collaboration and partnership across the University and to accelerate and enhance priority initiatives. The position also provides strategic input around the development of an equitable, diverse, and future-focused academic workforce. Priorities going forward are the implementation of key initiatives as part of the second phase of our Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences 2032 strategy ; the development of leadership capabilities in FASS; enhancing our external engagement activities and connections with industry, community and government, and ensuring the safety, health, and well-being of all staff and students. The appointee will hold PhD qualifications in a relevant FASS discipline with demonstrated research excellence and scholarship. They will bring a deep commitment to innovation, excellence in research and education, equity, diversity, inclusion, sustainability, and collaboration. They will have had significant experience leading academic units, including financial and operational management and strategic planning and delivery. They will have a mature understanding of workforce planning and management, research, education, and engagement in academic environments. They will also demonstrate a deep understanding of key strategic issues in the higher education and experience in the development and delivery of initiatives and projects therein.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The term of the appointment is five years on a full-time basis. The successful candidate will also be offered a professorial appointment in an appropriate discipline in the Faculty and will retain that position at the conclusion of their Deputy Deanship. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure/position description for further details. Candidate Brochure Pack: Candidate Brochure Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Jackie Besley from Devereaux Consulting at DeputyFASS@devereauxconsulting.com © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/School-Manager_0158263-1": {
      "title": "School Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term / continuing opportunity, located on the Darlington Campus, within the Faculty of Engineering Provide strategic and operational support to a School within the Faculty of Engineering Base Salary $146,197 - $154,260 (HEO9) p.a. + 17% superannuation      About the opportunity   Reporting to the Faculty General Manager, the School Manager will be a key member of the school leadership team, contributing to the planning, communication, and implementation of the school’s academic and operational strategies, goals, and initiatives.   In this role, you will lead the delivery of infrastructure, operational plans, and processes that support the school’s activities across education, research, research training, and external engagement. You will be accountable for operationalising strategic and business planning decisions, managing financial performance, strengthening stakeholder relationships, ensuring regulatory compliance and effective controls, and maintaining a safe and healthy workplace.   As a people leader, you will guide, develop, and support a team of professional staff while actively contributing to decision‑making processes within the school leadership team.   Some of your key responsibilities will include:   acting as a strategic partner to the Head of School, providing insight and advice to support effective decision‑making leading the formulation, communication, and implementation of the school’s strategic priorities providing strong operational leadership and effective day‑to‑day management across all school activities driving continuous improvement initiatives to enhance the school’s systems, processes, and service delivery fostering a culture of high performance, engagement, and accountability within the professional staff team overseeing financial management, governance, and internal controls to ensure sustainable and compliant operations optimising the allocation and utilisation of resources to support the school’s academic and operational goals ensuring adherence to the University’s governance frameworks, policies, and regulatory requirements leading risk management and ensuring a safe, healthy, and compliant work environment for all staff and students.     To learn more about the Faculty of Engineering click here .     About you   The University values courage and creativity; openness and engagement; inclusion and diversity; and respect and integrity. As such, we see the importance of recruiting talent aligned to these values and are looking for a School Manager who has:   a degree qualification in an applicable field (for example business administration) and/or equivalent combination of relevant training and experience exceptional interpersonal skills with the ability to develop and deploy effective working relationships across a diverse range of people excellent communication, consultation skills, and negotiation skills to drive internal and external engagement demonstrated track record in managing medium scale budgets and financial plans experience in business, infrastructure, operational and resource management experience in supporting the delivery of teaching and research within a university context.     To apply for this position, please explicitly address the questions below in a cover letter (which should be attached to your application):   Describe your experience of managing the operational functions of a business unit Describe your experience in people management and development.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Simon Drew, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Commercialisation-Associate_0157340-2": {
      "title": "Commercialisation Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nExciting opportunity to join the Commercialisation Office at the University of Sydney and help commercialise technologies in the Life Sciences, Medtech and Biotech sectors Full-time, continuing position Base Salary $117,133 - $127,610 + 17% Superannuation Flexible working arrangements   About the opportunity   The Commercialisation Office within the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) has an exciting opportunity for a Commercialisation Associate to join the team.   In this role you will work with the Faculty of Medicine and Health, one of the largest in the country, other Faculties and Research Partners to identify Intellectual Property (IP) suitable for commercial development and translation, develop and execute different commercialisation pathways.   As a Commercialisation Associate , you will support the Commercialisation Managers to develop and facilitate engagement between researchers, industry and investors to optimise the translation of the University's research, technology and expertise and execute related agreements with external organisations.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   work within a tight-knit team, reporting to a Commercialisation Manager managing a portfolio of Medtech and Biotech opportunities prepare preliminary due diligence evaluations for new invention disclosures, using a supported and proven framework support commercialisation projects through market and competitor analysis, finding comparable deals, preparing technology marketing flyers, investor opportunity disclosures and identifying potential licensees  analyse technical and scientific literature and reports and research budgets ensure that project files and relevant databases are kept up to date in line with protocol and policies undertake appropriate administrative duties required of working within a university environment.   About you   If you are driven, organised, have a commercial mindset and are able to support a large and varied portfolio of commercialisation projects, you are who we are looking for.     tertiary qualification in medicine, health or life sciences, biotechnology commercial experience the medtech, pharmaceuticals or biotech industry, or in a university tech transfer environment is highly desirable ability to foster an entrepreneurial culture and build partnerships between the research, business and government communities understanding of the innovation or commercialisation lifecycle high level of oral and written communication skills and ability to manage competing demands experience in or ability to review, develop and negotiate legal contracts understanding of intellectual property protection and management able to assess industrial applicability of advanced technology, associated business development and marketing of academic research   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kale Claffey Recruitment Operations by email to kale.claffey@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 19 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Learning-Experience-Designer-Graduate--Targeted-_0155880-2": {
      "title": "Learning Experience Designer Graduate (Targeted)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term role until end of June 2027 Be valued for your technical design knowledge within a collaborative team environment Base Salary $93,345 + 17% superannuation  Indigenous Targeted Role  This is a Targeted Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977.  About the opportunity The Learning Designer Graduate takes a learner-centred approach and contributes to projects that design, build, test, implement and evaluate educational experience that enhance the student experience. The successful applicant will draw on their recently acquired user experience knowledge and skills to contribute to the ongoing innovation of learning experiences and services. This position assists other designers to design and deliver solutions that facilitate the sustainable and scalable delivery of skill development and student experience programs. The Learning Designer Graduate will be a recent graduate of an undergraduate or postgraduate qualification in educational design, e-learning, design computing or user experience design. About you demonstrated capability to work with others to reach common goals, sharing information, supporting and building positive and constructive relationships strong problem solving and adaptability skills displaying independent judgement, initiative, and creativity that can be applied successfully in a collaborative team-based work environment sound organisational and analytical skills along with excellent communication, interpersonal, presentation and influencing skills demonstrated ability to successfully manage a busy workload and achieve agreed outcomes, with the ability to initiate projects and opportunities that have the potential to improve outcomes and performance of the work area demonstrated ability to maintain skills, knowledge and experience to innovate practices, processes and/or procedures at work the ability to work independently and understand and respond to changing priorities and trends in the Library and University environments demonstrated coding skills, with HTML5, CSS3 and JavaScript experience preferred. proficient with Adobe Creative Suite (Illustrator, Premier, InDesign) to create graphics, edit videos and design wireframes Why work with us  We are committed to creating a culturally safe, supportive and inclusive workplace for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander staff.  Key benefits include:   generous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements  access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding  participation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer support and professional development opportunities  YURA network (promoting sharing of experiences, and professional support for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander staff members)  tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Riviera Son, Recruitment Operations by email to riviera.son@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Research-Data-Storage-Specialist-_0158218-2": {
      "title": "Research Data Storage Specialist​",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSupport world-class research through enterprise-scale data storage and infrastructure services  Enable researchers to tackle complex data-intensive workloads and scientific challenges Salary range between $152,045 to $160,430 + 17% Superannuation    Solve by Talent is partnering with the University of Sydney to recruit a Research Data Storage Specialist within Information and Communications Technology (ICT). This role will contribute to the delivery and ongoing operation of research systems that support cutting-edge research activity across one of Australia’s leading universities.    Why join the University of Sydney?     Join one of Australia’s leading universities at a pivotal time, where technology is evolving to better support student learning, teaching excellence, and research at global scale.    ICT is central to the University’s mission, delivering the platforms, services, and partnerships that enable student success, strengthen teaching, and accelerate research impact.    With strong investment and ambitious transformation underway, you’ll work in a large and complex environment where your contribution will have visible, meaningful impact across the University community.    The opportunity   This role sits within the Research Infrastructure team, responsible for delivering and evolving the research infrastructure and data storage that underpin world-class research across the University.    You will play a key role in managing and evolving the University’s research infrastructure environment, working across on-premises and cloud-based platforms to ensure researchers have secure, scalable, resilient and effective technology services.  Working closely with researchers, ICT teams, vendors and service providers, you’ll help shape how research data and infrastructure are managed, protected and modernised across the institution, enabling researchers to tackle complex problems and drive innovation.    What you’ll do     Manage, optimise and evolve research data storage platforms and associated research infrastructure services, including compute and supporting systems.  Work with researchers and stakeholders to understand current and future infrastructure, storage and computing requirements and deliver fit-for-purpose solutions.  Support infrastructure and data lifecycle management, including capacity, performance, retention, archiving, accessibility and governance.  Contribute to backup, recovery, business continuity and disaster recovery strategies that support resilient and reliable research services.  Collaborate with vendors, managed service providers, Cyber Security and ICT teams to deliver secure, reliable and well-integrated research infrastructure services. Contribute to the planning, modernisation and continuous improvement of research infrastructure, storage and computing technologies. Evaluate emerging infrastructure, storage, cloud and advanced computing technologies and identify opportunities to enhance research capability. What we’re looking for Experience working in research organisations or institutions, with an understanding of research projects, workflows and lifecycles.  Experience managing and optimising enterprise data storage systems and associated infrastructure platforms, preferably within an academic or research environment.  Strong technical experience across research data storage, infrastructure and computing services, including cloud and on-premises environments.  Understanding of infrastructure and data lifecycle management, including capacity, performance, retention, archiving, data management and governance.  Understanding of security, access management, backup, recovery and resilience requirements within enterprise technology environments.  Experience evaluating or implementing infrastructure, storage, cloud or advanced computing technologies.  Strong stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with researchers, vendors, service providers and technical teams.    How to apply   If you're looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you.  Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the  Apply  button at the top of the page.      For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your  Workday  account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Accessibility and inclusion  The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you require adjustments or additional support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.  This may include adjustments to the interview format, additional time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you participate comfortably.    For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nick Barwick,  nick.barwick@sydney.edu.au .  ​  EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are  trust, accountability and excellence  and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to  diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People ,  women ,  people living with a disability ,  people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as  LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.      We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer.  Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      Pre-employment checks  Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia  You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Technical-Officer--Workshops---Mechatronics-_0157403-1": {
      "title": "Technical Officer (Workshops – Mechatronics)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position. Located at Susan Wakil Health Building (D18) on the Camperdown Campus Work with the team to design, assemble and troubleshoot specialised equipment and instrumentation for research and teaching Base Salary HEO6 $107,312 - $115,686 p.a. + 17% superannuation        About the opportunity     The Technical Support Services (TSS) Workshops team provides specialised design, fabrication and technical support services to teaching and research activities across the University. Working across multiple schools and faculties, the team partners with researchers and educators to develop custom equipment, prototypes and technical solutions that enable innovative teaching and research outcomes.   We are seeking a Technical Officer (Workshops – Mechatronics) to join this dynamic and collaborative team. In this hands-on role, you will work closely with clients to understand technical requirements, develop practical solutions and deliver bespoke equipment through a combination of design, prototyping, fabrication and systems integration. The Technical Officer (Workshops – Mechatronics) reports to the Project & Design Engineer, together they represent the wider TSS Workshops Camperdown Team in supporting clients in the Faculty of Medicine & Health. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a diverse range of projects, applying your engineering, prototyping and problem-solving skills to bring ideas from concept through to implementation.     Key responsibilities:   participate in consultation with researchers and teaching staff to identify requirements, evaluate design options and translate concepts into practical prototypes and technical solutions develop technical solutions through the integration of mechanical, electrical and electronic systems, including instrumentation, sensors, data acquisition and computer interfacing operate and maintain mechanical and electronic workshop equipment, including additive manufacturing, electronic benchtop testing instruments and general workshop tools support the Project & Design Engineer in selection and procurement of off-the shelf parts and components including motors, drivers and software contribute to the safe operation, maintenance and continuous improvement of workshop facilities, equipment and procedures work collaboratively with the wider TSS Workshops team to deliver high-quality technical services across the University About you:  qualification in mechatronics, electronics engineering, engineering technology, or an equivalent combination of relevant training and experience demonstrated experience designing, integrating, testing and troubleshooting mechanical and electronic systems demonstrated experience using benchtop test equipment including multimeters, oscilloscopes and power supplies experience using CAD software and rapid prototyping – using 3D printing and general workshop equipment working knowledge of sensors, instrumentation, data acquisition and control systems strong communication skills with the ability to build and maintain effective relationships with clients and colleagues demonstrated strong practical problem-solving skills, with the ability to develop innovative solutions from concept through to implementation. Work Rights You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to kevin.lim@sydney.edu.au . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Faculty-General-Manager_0158085-1": {
      "title": "Faculty General Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nLead the strategic and operational management of a world-class faculty, driving innovation and excellence across education, research, and engagement Located in Camperdown Campus Full-time, continuing opportunity with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at the University of Sydney is one of the largest and most preeminent arts faculties in Australia with six schools spanning the arts, humanities, and social sciences. The University of Sydney is consistently ranked as one of the top arts universities in Australia and the world and was recently ranked 1st in Australia for Arts and Humanities in the Times Higher Education (THE) World University Rankings by Subject 2026. The Faculty General Manager is a key member of the Faculty leadership team, responsible for delivering strategic and operational business management to support the Faculty’s academic, research, and engagement objectives. This role acts as the primary conduit between the Faculty and central services, ensuring alignment of operational strategies with the University’s overarching goals. Working closely with the Dean, the Faculty General Manager will support the implementation of the Faculty’s new strategic plan, build trusted relationships across the University, and drive cultural change that fosters collaboration and excellence. The Faculty General Manager will lead professional staff with vision and influence, empowering teams while navigating complex HR, operational, and education and research settings. You will lead the planning and implementation of infrastructure, financial, and resource strategies, while fostering a culture of collaboration and excellence. The position offers the opportunity to influence faculty-wide initiatives and contribute to the University’s vision of research and education excellence. The role reports to the Dean of Arts and Social Sciences, Professor Lisa Adkins. Your responsibilities Contribute to the execution of the Faculty’s strategic plan Oversee operational management, including infrastructure, systems, and service delivery Manage faculty budgets and financial performance in collaboration with the Dean and Finance Director Lead and mentor professional staff, ensuring effective workforce planning and development Provide strategic input into external engagement, marketing, and alumni relations Ensure compliance with governance, risk management, and WHS standards Drive continuous improvement and operational reform initiatives across the Faculty. About you (skills, capabilities and experience) We are looking for a dynamic leader with: Tertiary qualifications in business administration or a related field, or equivalent experience Extensive experience in operational and resource management within a large, complex organisation Proven ability to manage large-scale budgets and deliver financial sustainability Demonstrated success in leading transformational projects and change initiatives Strong interpersonal, negotiation, and communication skills In-depth understanding of the higher education sector and its strategic challenges. Term and remuneration The successful candidate will be offered a continuing position on a full-time basis. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the position description for further details. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sajad Bahram (Senior Talent Partner, Senior Talent Acquisition, Recruitment Operations) by email sajad.bahram@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Lecturer-in-Primary-English_0157871-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Primary English",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing role based at the Camperdown Campus in the Sydney School of Education and Social Work Opportunity to lead and innovate high-quality education at a leading global university·         Base Salary Level B $132,533 p.a. - $157,381 p.a. + 17% superannuation    About the opportunity  Join the Sydney School of Education and Social Work within the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, the Lecturer in Primary English role plays a key part in applying learning through research and scholarly activity to inform their education and research practice. They make significant contribution to teaching and learning practice, design and evaluation in initial teacher education programs while continuing to build their research contribution, expertise, and impact. Reporting to the Head of School , your key responsibilities will be:        develop and teach units in the area of literacy and English education in the primary education programs        build and maintain reputation of academic excellence make original and innovative contribution to research and education which expand knowledge or practice maintain a research profile which meets University expectations and standards development in research which enhance the reputation of the School, such as leading participation in internationally-funded research projects or partner-engaged research projects contribute to outstanding teaching and learning outcomes actively contribute to research, education and administrative activities contribute to collaborative teaching in the School and work flexibly and collaboratively in a team environment actively participate in governance and academic community at School and Faculty levels.     About you We are seeking an exceptional Lecturer in Primary English who has: a PhD in the area of literacy education, particularly primary English education or a related field experience in teaching and learning, as evidenced by participation in team teaching within an established program and experience in teaching in a variety of settings demonstrated success in designing, implementing and evaluating teaching and learning initiatives developing network of relationships with key academic, industry and professional institutions, partners and stakeholders experience in supervising or mentoring others and/or coordinating team activities a developing knowledge and understanding of the key strategic issues in the discipline and higher education sector a developing network of academic industry and professional partners and stakeholders.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations    Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .      How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Helen Efstathiou, Recruitment Consultant, Recruitment Operations on +61 2 8627 7137 or by email helen.efstathiou@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.   Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/IT-Support-Technician_0157953": {
      "title": "IT Support Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nCasual opportunity for an IT Support Technician Make a real impact: Use your technical expertise to support staff and students, solve complex IT challenges and deliver exceptional customer service in a dynamic university environment. HEO 6 $73.45- $79.18 per hour + super   Why join the University of Sydney? Join one of Australia's leading universities at a pivotal time, where technology is evolving to better support student learning, teaching excellence, and research at global scale. ICT is central to the University's mission, delivering the platforms, services, and partnerships that enable student success, strengthen teaching, and accelerate research impact. With strong investment and ambitious transformation underway, you'll work in a large and complex environment where your contribution will have visible, meaningful impact across the University community.   The opportunity The IT Support Technician – Field Services is responsible for providing technical support services with client technology to staff and students at the University. The position is a key customer interface for ICT’s delivery of in-field services, working under general direction as a routine contact point for receiving, handling, and resolving a broad range of support incidents and requests.   What you'll do Provide professional, reliable technical support to University staff and students across a range of client technologies. Manage and resolve IT incidents and service requests within agreed service levels (SLAs), keeping customers informed throughout the process. Investigate and troubleshoot hardware, software, systems and network issues to identify effective solutions. Deliver expert technical advice and guidance on the use of client technologies across Windows, Apple, iOS and Android environments. Maintain accurate service records, contribute to knowledge bases and develop operational and training documentation. Collaborate with ICT teams, University colleagues and external partners to deliver customer-focused service excellence and continuous improvement.   What we're looking for Demonstrated experience providing customer-focused IT support in a professional or enterprise environment. Strong technical troubleshooting and problem-solving skills across Windows, Apple, iOS and Android technologies. Experience managing IT incidents and service requests using Service Management software and remote support tools. Knowledge of Microsoft Exchange Online, Intune, Jamf and Azure in a large-scale environment. Experience troubleshooting network issues, including TCP/IP, DNS and DHCP, with enterprise software installation and deployment. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with a commitment to delivering service excellence and working collaboratively with ICT teams. How to apply If you're looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you. Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   Accessibility and inclusion The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you require adjustments or additional support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.   This may include adjustments to the interview format, additional time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you participate comfortably. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Jenny.Seo@sydney.edu.au   EEO statement  At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .     Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.   Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Thursday 15 October 2026 11:59 PM"
